UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions.' In the Lewis model, an acid is any species that accepts a pair of electrons to form a coordinate covalent bond, while a base is the species that donates that pair; identifying the acid therefore requires locating the species with a vacant orbital rather than reading off charges or electronegativities. (Choice B) is correct: boron in \(\text{BF}_3\) has only six valence electrons (three \(\text{B}\text{-}\text{F}\) bonds and no lone pair), so it has an empty valence orbital and can accept the lone pair on nitrogen, making \(\text{BF}_3\) the electron-pair acceptor and thus the Lewis acid. (Choice A) is incorrect: nitrogen in \(\text{NH}_3\) does hold the lone pair used to form the bond, but donating that pair is the defining behavior of a Lewis base, so this feature identifies \(\text{NH}_3\) as the base, not the acid. (Choice C) is incorrect: it also mislabels \(\text{NH}_3\) as the acid, and although nitrogen is more electronegative than boron, pulling electron density is not the same as accepting a lone pair into an empty orbital, and \(\text{NH}_3\) is in fact the donor. (Choice D) is incorrect: it reaches the right species but for a false reason. Fluorine, not boron, is the electronegative element in \(\text{BF}_3\); boron acts as the acid because of its empty orbital and incomplete octet, not because it is electron-rich. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the Lewis acid-base definition to a novel reactant pair and reason from each species' electron structure to assign the acceptor role.$px$ WHERE id = 'ce64d613-c297-4b9c-87e6-899a504dc5d7';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Ions in Solutions (GC).' The naming system for oxyanions (per-, -ate, -ite, hypo-) fixes only the relative ordering of oxygen atoms within a single element's series; it does not set a universal oxygen count or a universal charge, because those depend on the central atom. As the text notes, the number of oxygens tied to a given suffix is not consistent across elements (nitrate is \(\text{NO}_3^{-}\) while sulfate is \(\text{SO}_4^{2-}\)), so each familiar ion's formula and charge must be known. Phosphate, the "-ate" ion of phosphorus, is \(\text{PO}_4^{3-}\). (Choice A) is correct: phosphate carries four oxygen atoms and a 3- charge, the value the AAMC lists among the familiar ions to know; nothing in the stem's sulfate or chlorate data supplies this, so it must be recalled and applied. (Choice B) is incorrect: \(\text{PO}_3^{-}\) copies chlorate's three-oxygen, 1- pattern onto phosphorus, the exact "all -ate ions match" error the stem flags; phosphate is not the phosphorus analog of chlorate. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(\text{PO}_4^{2-}\) borrows sulfate's 2- charge while keeping four oxygens, mixing a correct oxygen count with the wrong charge for the phosphorus center. (Choice D) is incorrect: \(\text{PO}_4^{-}\) keeps four oxygens but assigns a 1- charge, treating every "-ate" ion as singly charged the way chlorate and nitrate are. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that oxyanion suffixes fix only relative oxygen ordering, not absolute oxygen count or charge, and then recall the correct formula and charge of a familiar ion whose value the stem never provides.$px$ WHERE id = 'ce88e2db-676d-4e25-985b-94e2ebde1317';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is A because carbohydrate attachment to a secreted glycoprotein first occurs in the rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER), and the molecule's final step is exocytosis when a Golgi-derived secretory vesicle fuses with the plasma membrane. As a ribosome on the RER translates the enzyme, the polypeptide enters the RER lumen, where it undergoes structural modifications, including the initial attachment of a carbohydrate to the protein. The protein then travels by transport vesicle to the Golgi cis face, where additional carbohydrates are added and the protein is sorted and tagged. Finally it is packaged into secretory vesicles that bud from the Golgi's trans face, and these vesicles fuse with the plasma membrane and release their contents outside the cell. (Choice A) Correct: the carbohydrate is added in the ER lumen first and the Golgi later adds more, so the FIRST attachment site is the RER; exocytosis of a Golgi-derived secretory vesicle is indeed the terminal step for a secreted enzyme. (Choice B) Partial-truth trap: the Golgi genuinely glycosylates proteins (one of its most frequent modifications is adding short chains of sugar molecules), but it adds MORE carbohydrate to a chain that began in the ER; because the stem asks where the carbohydrate is FIRST acquired, the Golgi is the site of subsequent, not initial, glycosylation. (Choice C) Reversed/incorrect destination: lysosomes are the cell's degradative compartment, whose acidic hydrolytic enzymes break down macromolecules and worn-out organelles; they degrade intracellular material rather than fusing with the plasma membrane to release a functional secreted enzyme, so this misroutes the exocytic step. (Choice D) Process-step confusion: free cytoplasmic ribosomes synthesize the polypeptide, but glycosylation does not occur on the ribosome itself. The protein must first be transferred into the RER lumen, where modifications such as carbohydrate attachment take place, so the carbohydrate cannot be acquired on a cytoplasmic ribosome. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must integrate the sequence of endomembrane events and apply it to predict where carbohydrate is first added and what the terminal secretory step is.$px$ WHERE id = 'ce94b73f-ef81-4c9f-9fdb-3bdaf3121eef';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$Two homologous enzymes catalyze the same reaction: one from a mesophilic bacterium and one from a thermophilic bacterium that grows near hydrothermal vents. Each is assayed for catalytic rate across 0 to 90 °C, and separately its fraction of unfolded protein is measured by a spectroscopic melting assay. The results are tabulated below.

| Enzyme | Catalytic rate peaks at | 50% of protein unfolded at | Rate falls to ~0 by |
|--------|-------------------------|----------------------------|---------------------|
| Mesophilic | 40 °C | 46 °C | 55 °C |
| Thermophilic | 75 °C | 82 °C | 88 °C |

For the thermophilic enzyme, the researcher wants to report the single parameter that a physician-scientist could use to predict the temperature at which this enzyme would work fastest inside its host organism. Which value should be reported, and by what term?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' Enzyme catalytic rate rises with temperature up to a peak and then falls sharply as heat unfolds (denatures) the protein; from roughly 0 to about 40 or 50 °C enzyme activity usually increases along with most reaction rates, and at higher temperatures activity decreases dramatically as the enzyme denatures. The temperature at which activity is maximal is the enzyme's optimal temperature, and thermophilic enzymes are shifted to higher optima than their mesophilic homologs. (Choice A) is correct: the value that predicts where the enzyme works fastest is the peak of the rate-versus-temperature curve, which for the thermophilic enzyme is 75 °C, and that peak is by definition the optimal temperature. (Choice B) is incorrect: 88 °C is where the thermophilic enzyme's rate has fallen to nearly zero because the protein is essentially fully denatured; this denaturation endpoint marks the loss of activity, not the temperature of fastest catalysis. (Choice C) is incorrect: 82 °C is the melting temperature (Tm), the point at which 50% of the protein population is unfolded. That is a stability parameter describing the unfolding transition, and it lies above and is distinct from the activity peak. (Choice D) is incorrect: 46 °C is the mesophilic homolog's Tm, not any value from the thermophilic enzyme, and it is mislabeled as an optimal temperature; picking it confuses the two enzymes and confuses unfolding with peak activity. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between temperature, catalytic rate, and protein unfolding to select the correct value from data and match it to the term optimal temperature, distinguishing it from denaturation and melting temperatures.$px$ WHERE id = 'ceaa5293-654d-48b7-9c77-08d2eaf87379';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Metabolism of Fatty Acids and Proteins.'

The answer is B because the stem supplies the governing principle: density rises with protein fraction because protein is denser than lipid. Particle X is 22% protein, while Particle Y is only 6% protein. The particle with the larger share of the denser component (protein) must be the denser particle overall, so Particle X is denser. This follows directly from the supplied composition and the density principle, with no need to recall any lipoprotein class name.

(Choice B) is correct: Particle X has the higher protein fraction (22% vs 6%), and since protein is the denser component, the higher-protein particle is denser.
(Choice A) is incorrect: it treats lipid as the denser component. The stem states protein is denser than lipid, so a higher lipid fraction lowers density rather than raising it.
(Choice C) is incorrect: it reaches the right particle for the wrong reason and also misreads the composition. Particle X has the smaller, not larger, lipid fraction, and a larger lipid fraction would lower density.
(Choice D) is incorrect: it correctly notes Particle Y's lower protein fraction but draws the wrong conclusion; a lower protein fraction means less of the denser component, so Particle Y is the less dense particle.

As a Skill 4 (reasoning about data and research) item, it requires applying a stated density-composition relationship to the supplied percentages rather than recalling memorized particle densities.$px$ WHERE id = 'cec05f2f-6d03-449e-ae09-2592c6717821';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A researcher studies a purified enzyme. Using a technique that measures the mass of the protein in its intact, folded state, she finds a single species of about 200 kDa. She then runs the same purified sample on SDS-PAGE (a gel that first heats the protein in an ionic detergent to unfold every chain) and observes four bands, each at about 50 kDa. No reducing agent was needed to produce the four bands. Which level of protein organization does the difference between these two results reveal about this enzyme?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The intact protein has a mass of ~200 kDa, but a denaturing gel that unfolds and separates individual polypeptide chains yields four equal ~50 kDa pieces (4 × 50 ≈ 200), and because no reducing agent was required, the pieces were not linked by disulfide bonds. Subunits held together only by noncovalent interactions separate under denaturing conditions such as SDS, so a large native mass that resolves into several smaller equal masses under SDS identifies a multi-subunit assembly. (Choice A) is incorrect: SDS does unfold tertiary structure, but unfolding a single chain does not cut it into four separate 50 kDa pieces. A single polypeptide would still migrate as one band at ~200 kDa, so the four-band result cannot reflect merely the 3-D folding of one chain. (Choice B) is correct: the drop from one 200 kDa native species to four equal 50 kDa chains, separable without breaking any covalent bonds, is the signature of quaternary structure: the overall arrangement that arises when separate protein chains associate into a single assembly, with the subunits held together by noncovalent interactions. (Choice C) is incorrect: SDS also disrupts the H-bonds of secondary structure, but losing helices and sheets unfolds a chain without dividing it into multiple bands; secondary structure describes backbone H-bonding within a chain, not the number of chains present. (Choice D) is incorrect: SDS leaves peptide bonds intact, so each 50 kDa band does retain its primary sequence, but primary structure is simply the linear amino acid sequence of one chain and cannot explain why the native protein is four times as large. The relationship between the 200 kDa and 50 kDa results, not the sequence itself, is what the data reveal. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definitions of the four structural levels to native- versus denaturing-mass data, inferring from a large native mass that resolves into equal noncovalently linked subunits that the protein possesses quaternary structure.$px$ WHERE id = 'cecdc170-c2c0-41fb-8be4-fc16da6d6d64';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Titration (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because the equivalence point is reached when the moles of added acid equal the moles of base originally present, and that mole count is fixed entirely by the stoichiometry of the samples. Each sample contains \((0.0250\,\text{L})(0.10\,\text{M}) = 2.5\times10^{-3}\,\text{mol}\) of base, so each requires \(2.5\times10^{-3}\,\text{mol}\) of \(\text{HCl}\), which at \(0.10\,\text{M}\) is exactly \(25.0\,\text{mL}\) in both cases. The strength of the base governs the pH at the equivalence point (the ammonia titration gives an acidic equivalence point because \(\text{NH}_4^+\) remains, while the \(\text{NaOH}\) titration gives a neutral one), but it does not change the volume of titrant needed to get there. (Choice A) The \(K_b\) value affects only the pH profile of the curve and the pH at equivalence, not the equivalence volume, which is determined solely by the moles of base present. (Choice B) This reflects the common misconception that partial ionization reduces the moles available to react; in fact, as protons are removed by the acid, the equilibrium continually replenishes the reactive base, so the full \(2.5\times10^{-3}\,\text{mol}\) is ultimately neutralized. (Choice C) This is the opposite misconception, assuming extra titrant is consumed to overcome incomplete ionization, but the stoichiometric mole ratio, not the rate or extent of ionization, sets the endpoint volume. Skill 4$px$ WHERE id = 'cedd10b2-d4dc-421f-9d85-6b0e943414bc';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is C because the acidic side-chain class is defined by side chains carrying a carboxylic acid functional group, and only two amino acids meet this definition. Glutamic acid (glutamate) and aspartic acid (aspartate) constitute the acidic amino acids: their side chains contain carboxylic acid functional groups capable of fully ionizing in solution, so Aspartate (Asp, D) and Glutamate (Glu, E) are grouped together as the acidic amino acids and carry a net negative charge. (Choice A) is incorrect: lysine and arginine are basic, not acidic. The basic amino acids (lysine, arginine, and histidine) contain amine functional groups that can be protonated to carry a full charge, so they carry positive, not negative, side-chain charge. (Choice B) is incorrect: asparagine and glutamine are the amide-containing members of the polar uncharged class, and despite resembling Asp/Glu in name and one-letter proximity, their amide side chains do not ionize. (Choice C) is correct: aspartate and glutamate are the only two amino acids whose side chains contain a carboxylic acid group, placing them in the acidic class. (Choice D) is incorrect: serine and threonine are hydroxyl-containing members of the polar uncharged class, and their side chains are uncharged. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall which amino acids constitute the acidic side-chain class.$px$ WHERE id = 'cfd11d63-fa78-4318-a9af-57e8519f14ef';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC).' A disproportionation reaction is a redox process in which the same element functions as both the oxidant and the reductant, so one element's oxidation number must both increase and decrease within the single equation. To identify one, you assign oxidation numbers to each element on both sides and look for a single element whose oxidation number splits in two directions. (Choice A) is correct: chlorine begins as \(\text{Cl}_2\) with an oxidation number of \(0\); some chlorine is reduced to \(\text{Cl}^-\) (oxidation number \(-1\)) while the rest is oxidized to \(\text{ClO}_3^-\), where chlorine is \(+5\). Because the same element (chlorine) is simultaneously oxidized and reduced, this is a disproportionation. (Choice B) is incorrect: this is a redox reaction, but two different elements change. Aluminum is oxidized (\(0 \rightarrow +3\)) and bromine is reduced (\(0 \rightarrow -1\)); no single element is both oxidized and reduced, so it is not a disproportionation. (Choice C) is incorrect: this displacement reaction is redox, yet the changes occur in two separate elements. Zinc is oxidized (\(0 \rightarrow +2\)) and copper is reduced (\(+2 \rightarrow 0\)); one element replacing another is not the same as one element splitting into two oxidation states. (Choice D) is incorrect: although one reactant breaks into two products, every element keeps its oxidation number (Ca is \(+2\), C is \(+4\), O is \(-2\) on both sides), so this is not even a redox reaction, let alone a disproportionation. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply oxidation-number assignments across each equation to determine which one has a single element that is both oxidized and reduced.$px$ WHERE id = 'd0736631-b2f1-4c15-aa31-51f2e34f4c29';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$After a carbohydrate-rich meal, insulin rises and glucose uptake into resting skeletal muscle and adipocytes increases several-fold within a few minutes. In a cell-culture experiment, this rapid rise still occurs when transcription and translation are blocked, and antibody labeling shows that the total amount of GLUT4 protein per cell is unchanged before and after insulin. What changes is that GLUT4 shifts from the cytoplasm to the plasma membrane. Which explanation best accounts for how insulin raises glucose uptake so quickly?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is A because insulin raises glucose uptake into skeletal muscle and adipose tissue on a timescale of minutes by relocating transporters that already exist, not by making new ones. When insulin binds its cell-surface receptor, the downstream signaling cascade triggers GLUT4-containing storage vesicles to fuse with the plasma membrane, increasing the number of GLUT4 transporters at the cell surface so that facilitated glucose entry climbs almost at once. The experiment nails the mechanism: the rise still occurs when transcription and translation are blocked, and the total amount of GLUT4 per cell is unchanged. Only its location shifts from cytoplasm to membrane. Because no new protein is required, the response is fast, and it reverses just as quickly when insulin falls and the transporters are internalized again.

(Choice A) is correct: insulin recruits pre-formed GLUT4 from intracellular vesicles to the surface, raising transporter number within minutes without new synthesis, exactly matching the unchanged total GLUT4 and the shift in location.
(Choice B) is incorrect: building new transporters requires transcription and translation, yet the stem shows the rise persists when both are blocked and the total amount of GLUT4 does not change, so newly synthesized protein cannot explain the minutes-fast response.
(Choice C) is incorrect: the data show GLUT4 moving from cytoplasm to the membrane (the number of surface transporters increases), so the effect is not merely speeding up transporters already at the surface while their number stays constant.
(Choice D) is incorrect: pulling GLUT4 off the surface into storage vesicles would lower glucose entry, the opposite of the observed shift toward the plasma membrane and the observed rise in uptake.

This question tests Skill 2 (Scientific Reasoning and Problem-Solving) because it asks you to infer insulin's mechanism from experimental observations (an unchanged total transporter amount, a shift in transporter location, and persistence when synthesis is blocked) rather than recalling a memorized fact.$px$ WHERE id = 'd14c6b18-decf-4c7d-b058-7d1e4df1c1c4';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Early Embryonic Development'. The answer is C because cleavage is a series of mitotic divisions that subdivide the zygote into progressively smaller cells without any net growth, so the multicellular embryo remains about the same size as the original egg. Each successive cleavage partitions the egg into smaller and smaller cells with no actual cell growth, so the 8-cell stage and the blastula are roughly the same size as the original zygote. Although each cleavage produces more cells, it does not increase the total volume of the conceptus. Therefore the cell count climbs (1 to 4 to 8) while the summed volume of all cells stays roughly constant. (Choice A) Incorrect; because cleavage involves no actual cell growth, the total volume does not double each round. This confuses cleavage with growth. (Choice B) Incorrect; the blastomeres get progressively smaller and do not grow back to the original size between divisions. (Choice C) Correct; the embryo is subdivided into more, smaller blastomeres while the total volume remains about the same size as the original zygote. (Choice D) Incorrect; cleavage continues past the 8-cell stage to the morula and blastula, and the embryo does not enlarge by enlarging existing cells. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling the defining feature of cleavage: division without growth.$px$ WHERE id = 'd192da50-cfa8-44db-9459-70cdac7f2396';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Separations and Purifications.' The stationary-to-mobile ratio reported for each compound is a partition ratio: how strongly the compound sits on the stationary phase versus how much of it travels dissolved in the moving mobile phase. A compound spends time bound to the stationary phase (not moving) and time in the mobile phase (moving with the flow), so the fraction of time it spends in the mobile phase sets how fast it travels down the column. A large stationary-to-mobile ratio means the compound is mostly stuck to the stationary phase, moves little, and takes a long time to reach the end (long retention). A small ratio means the compound rides mostly in the mobile phase, moves quickly, and exits early. Ranking by the ratio: P = 0.4 (smallest, mostly mobile, fastest), R = 1.2 (intermediate), Q = 3.5 (largest, mostly stationary, slowest). Elution order from first-out to last-out is therefore P, then R, then Q.

The answer is A because compounds elute in order of increasing affinity for the stationary phase: the one that partitions most into the mobile phase (P, ratio 0.4) migrates fastest and exits first, followed by R (1.2), and finally Q (3.5), which clings to the stationary phase and is retained longest.

(Choice A) is correct: it orders the compounds by increasing stationary-phase affinity (0.4 < 1.2 < 3.5), which is the same as decreasing mobile-phase residence, so the fastest-migrating compound (P) elutes first and the most-retained compound (Q) elutes last.

(Choice B) is incorrect: this reverses the underlying rule. It treats a high stationary-to-mobile ratio as meaning fast migration, putting Q (3.5) first. In reality, strong binding to the stationary phase holds a compound back, so Q is retained longest and elutes last, not first.

(Choice C) is incorrect: this puts R first, as if the intermediate compound eluted before P. P has the smallest ratio (0.4) and thus the greatest tendency to stay in the mobile phase, so P must migrate faster than R and elute before it. The relative positions of R and P are swapped here.

(Choice D) is incorrect: it leads with Q, the compound with the largest stationary-to-mobile ratio (3.5) and therefore the strongest binding to the stationary phase. This confuses the most-retained compound (the last peak to exit) with the first to elute. High stationary-phase affinity produces the longest retention, so Q must elute last, not first.

This question requires Skill 2 (Scientific Reasoning and Problem-Solving) because the stem supplies partition data and asks you to apply the governing principle of chromatography (migration speed is set by how a compound distributes between the stationary and mobile phases) to predict an experimental outcome rather than recall a definition.$px$ WHERE id = 'd1b5a4b7-3993-4045-b22f-1f312b04300f';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC).' For a weak acid only a small fraction of molecules ionize, and the equilibrium \(\text{HA} + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightleftharpoons \text{H}_3\text{O}^+ + \text{A}^-\) shifts when the solution is diluted. Because \(K_a\) is fixed at a given temperature, adding water shifts the equilibrium toward the products (a larger ionized fraction), yet the ions produced are spread through a much larger volume, so the actual hydronium concentration still falls. (Choice A) is correct: the percent ionization of a weak acid typically increases as the acid is diluted; here it rises from about \(1.3\%\) at \(0.10\,\text{M}\) to about \(4.2\%\) at \(0.010\,\text{M}\), while \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+]\) falls from roughly \(1.3\times10^{-3}\,\text{M}\) to \(4.2\times10^{-4}\,\text{M}\). (Choice B) is incorrect: although the percent ionized does increase, the larger fraction cannot compensate for the tenfold dilution, so the actual hydronium concentration drops rather than rises. (Choice C) is incorrect: this reverses the true trend. Percent ionization increases, not decreases, on dilution, even though the student correctly senses that \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+]\) falls. (Choice D) is incorrect: a constant \(K_a\) does not mean a constant percent ionization; unlike \(K_a\), percent ionization depends on the initial concentration and changes as the solution is diluted. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the weak-acid ionization equilibrium to a novel dilution scenario, distinguishing the ionized fraction from the absolute hydronium concentration.$px$ WHERE id = 'd1fd8c9c-1062-486a-9edf-0c03f566e77e';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$In a patient with chronically elevated blood volume, a circulating peptide hormone is measured that acts on the kidneys to reduce sodium reabsorption (increasing sodium and water excretion) while also suppressing renin release; its net effect is to lower blood volume and blood pressure. This peptide is secreted by which of the following non-classic endocrine organs?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.' The answer is D because the hormone described is atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP): it promotes renal sodium and water excretion, suppresses renin, and thereby lowers blood volume and blood pressure. ANP is secreted by specialized cells in the heart, and the combination of a natriuretic effect plus renin suppression is the signature of ANP and identifies the heart as the source organ. (Choice D) is correct: the heart is a non-classic endocrine organ whose atrial cells release ANP in response to high blood volume, and ANP's actions include increased renal sodium/water excretion, inhibition of renin, and a resulting decrease in blood volume and pressure. (Choice B) is incorrect: the kidneys are the target tissue on which this peptide acts, not its source; the kidneys' own hormones in this context are renin (which the described peptide suppresses) and erythropoietin, which governs red-cell production, so mistaking the effector organ for the signal source is the trap here. (Choice C) is incorrect: the adrenal cortex releases aldosterone, which promotes sodium reabsorption (retention), the opposite direction of the described natriuretic effect. Its RAAS output is inhibited rather than triggered by this pathway, so it cannot be the source of a natriuretic peptide. (Choice A) is incorrect: the thymus is a non-classic endocrine organ, but it secretes thymosins that support T-lymphocyte development and has no role in sodium/water balance or renin regulation. Skill 2 is demonstrated by linking the dual natriuretic-plus-renin-suppressing effect to the correct source organ, distinguishing the hormone's origin from the tissue on which it acts.$px$ WHERE id = 'd2281960-a5b9-4adf-a210-64796ded88db';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is A because the problem must be solved in three chained steps. Step 1 (recover the starting composition): the given \(\text{pH}\) is not equal to the \(\text{p}K_a\), so the buffer is not equimolar, and the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation must be inverted to find the ratio: \(\log\!\left(\frac{[\text{NH}_3]}{[\text{NH}_4^+]}\right) = \text{pH} - \text{p}K_a = 9.55 - 9.25 = 0.30\), so \(\frac{[\text{NH}_3]}{[\text{NH}_4^+]} = 10^{0.30} = 2.0\). Combining the ratio \(2{:}1\) with the constraint that the two species total \(0.60\ \text{mol}\) gives \([\text{NH}_3] = 0.40\ \text{mol}\) and \([\text{NH}_4^+] = 0.20\ \text{mol}\). Step 2 (strong-acid stoichiometry): the \(0.25\ \text{mol}\ \text{H}^+\) reacts essentially completely with the base, \(\text{NH}_3 + \text{H}^+ \rightarrow \text{NH}_4^+\); because \(0.25\ \text{mol}\ \text{H}^+ < 0.40\ \text{mol}\ \text{NH}_3\), the base is the limiting reactant but is not fully consumed, leaving \([\text{NH}_3] = 0.40 - 0.25 = 0.15\ \text{mol}\) and \([\text{NH}_4^+] = 0.20 + 0.25 = 0.45\ \text{mol}\). Step 3 (reapply Henderson-Hasselbalch): \(\text{pH} = 9.25 + \log\!\left(\frac{0.15}{0.45}\right) = 9.25 + \log\!\left(\frac{1}{3}\right) = 9.25 + (-0.48) = 8.77\). (Choice B) \(9.73\) comes from carrying out the correct stoichiometry but inverting the logarithm term, \(9.25 + \log\!\left(\frac{0.45}{0.15}\right) = 9.25 + \log(3)\); placing the conjugate acid over the base reverses the direction of the shift, so adding acid would appear to raise the \(\text{pH}\), which is impossible. (Choice C) \(9.55\) is simply the initial \(\text{pH}\) reported unchanged; it follows from completing Step 1 but omitting the neutralization, ignoring that the added \(\text{H}^+\) shifts the ratio toward the conjugate acid and must lower the \(\text{pH}\). (Choice D) \(8.21\) results from skipping Step 1 and assuming the buffer started equimolar (splitting the \(0.60\ \text{mol}\) total evenly into \(0.30\ \text{mol}\) of each species), then neutralizing to \(0.05\ \text{mol}\ \text{NH}_3\) and \(0.55\ \text{mol}\ \text{NH}_4^+\) for \(9.25 + \log\!\left(\frac{0.05}{0.55}\right)\); this discards the controlling constraint that the given \(\text{pH}\) of \(9.55 eq\text{p}K_a\) fixes a \(2{:}1\) starting ratio, not a \(1{:}1\) one. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must invert the Henderson-Hasselbalch relation to recover an unknown buffer composition, feed that result through a limiting-reactant neutralization, and reapply the relation to obtain the final \(\text{pH}\).$px$ WHERE id = 'd2c0b2c1-669f-411c-a58c-376cec2fe17f';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Atoms, nuclear decay, electronic structure, and atomic chemical behavior'. The answer to this question is D because the detector selects ions by mass-to-charge ratio, not by mass alone. Solving \( r = \frac{1}{B}\sqrt{\frac{2Vm}{q}} \) for the focused ratio gives \( \frac{m}{q} = \frac{B^2 r^2}{2V} \), so with \( V \), \( r \), and the detector position fixed, \( B \propto \sqrt{m/q} \). The controlling constraint is the charge: \( ^{90}\text{Zr}^{+} \) presents \( m/q = 90/1 = 90 \), but the target \( ^{96}\text{Zr}^{2+} \) presents \( m/q = 96/2 = 48 \), a smaller ratio despite the larger mass. Scaling the field by \( \sqrt{48/90} \) gives \( B = 0.480\ \text{T} \times \sqrt{48/90} = 0.480 \times 0.730 = 0.351\ \text{T} \), a lower field. (Choice A) This inverts the dependence, reasoning that a smaller \( m/q \) needs a larger \( B \); since \( B \propto \sqrt{m/q} \), a smaller ratio requires a smaller field, so applying \( \sqrt{90/48} \) drives \( B \) the wrong direction to \( 0.657\ \text{T} \). (Choice B) This treats the second ion as singly charged \( ^{96}\text{Zr}^{+} \) and applies \( \sqrt{96/90} \); it captures the correct \( B \propto \sqrt{m/q} \) form but drops the \( 2+ \) charge, so it raises \( B \) to \( 0.496\ \text{T} \) when the doubling of \( q \) actually lowers \( m/q \) and the field. (Choice C) This uses the correct \( m/q \) values of \( 48 \) and \( 90 \) but scales \( B \) linearly with the ratio rather than with its square root; because \( m/q \propto B^2 \), the field must change by \( \sqrt{48/90} \), not \( 48/90 \), so \( 0.256\ \text{T} \) overshoots the reduction. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain the isotope-and-charge identification to the correct \( m/q \) values, recognize that charge state governs the discriminator, and carry the squared-field relationship through a multi-step calculation to the correct magnitude and direction.$px$ WHERE id = 'd31063ad-66a4-4020-9422-89b76fb1a987';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is C because the \(\text{pI}\) is found by averaging the \(\text{pK}_a\) values of the two groups closest to and on either side of the net-neutral species. Tracking the charge as base is added: at low \(\text{pH}\) all groups are protonated, so the \(\alpha\)-carboxyl is neutral, the side-chain carboxyl is neutral, and the \(\alpha\)-amino is \(-\text{NH}_3^+\), giving a net charge of \(+1\). Deprotonating the lowest \(\text{pK}_a\) group (the \(\alpha\)-carboxyl, \(\text{pK}_a = 2.0\)) converts it to \(-\text{COO}^-\), producing the net-neutral zwitterion (\(-1\) from \(\alpha\)-\(\text{COO}^-\), \(+1\) from \(-\text{NH}_3^+\)). The very next deprotonation (the side-chain carboxyl, \(\text{pK}_a = 4.0\)) drives the molecule to a net charge of \(-1\). The zero-charge form is therefore bracketed by \(\text{pK}_a = 2.0\) and \(\text{pK}_a = 4.0\), so \(\text{pI} = (2.0 + 4.0)/2 = 3.0\). Those are the two lowest \(\text{pK}_a\) values, exactly as expected for an acidic amino acid. (Choice A) 4.0 and 9.5 are the \(\text{pK}_a\) values that flank the \(-1\) species, not the neutral one; averaging them gives \(6.75\), which is the \(\text{pH}\) of half-charge between the \(-1\) and \(-2\) forms, not the \(\text{pI}\). (Choice B) 2.0 and 9.5 are the standard pair for a neutral amino acid with no ionizable side chain, but here the acidic side chain deprotonates before the amino group, so the neutral form is no longer flanked by the \(\alpha\)-amino \(\text{pK}_a\). (Choice C) is correct: 2.0 and 4.0 bracket the zero-charge species, so they are the two \(\text{pK}_a\) values averaged. (Choice D) the \(\text{pI}\) is always the average of exactly the two \(\text{pK}_a\) values flanking the neutral form; averaging all three has no defined meaning and ignores which species actually carries zero charge. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply protonation-state reasoning to identify which two ionizations flank the net-neutral form and then average those two \(\text{pK}_a\) values.$px$ WHERE id = 'd33c342a-36c6-4d91-ab3c-4b06630a917a';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.' In eukaryotes, a pre-mRNA contains noncoding introns that must be precisely removed by spliceosomes, which recognize specific sequences marking each intron's boundaries (GU at the 5' end and AG at the 3' end); the exons are then rejoined to form the mature mRNA. Although the internal sequence of an intron can often be mutated without altering the final protein, the splicing itself must be exact: if the process errs by even a single nucleotide, the reading frame of the rejoined exons shifts and the resulting protein is dysfunctional. (Choice A) is correct: by inserting extra DNA within the intron, the transposable element corrupts the splice-site sequences the spliceosome depends on, so the intron is retained or mis-excised, the exons are joined incorrectly, and the resulting OCA2 mRNA cannot encode a functional protein. (Choice B) is incorrect: it captures the common assumption that any change to a noncoding intron is harmless, but this insertion is precisely the exception. It damages the splicing signals, so the "noncoding" location does not spare the protein. (Choice C) is incorrect: a missense substitution swaps one amino acid for another, but that requires a change within an exon; the element sits in an intron, which is removed before translation, so it does not directly rewrite a codon in the protein. (Choice D) is incorrect: relocating a gene to another chromosome describes translocation, which moves existing DNA rather than inserting new material into this intron, and here the gene is still in place while its transcript is mis-processed. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the mechanism of pre-mRNA splicing to infer why an insertion in a noncoding intron, not a coding exon, still yields a nonfunctional protein.$px$ WHERE id = 'd36e0152-2f39-47ec-99f8-4ac82f6aadd8';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$Consider the following phosphoryl-transfer potential ranking (most exergonic phosphate hydrolysis at the top): phosphoenolpyruvate (PEP) and creatine phosphate rank ABOVE ATP; ATP is intermediate; glucose-6-phosphate ranks BELOW ATP. A student argues that because ATP is only a mid-level phosphate donor, an even higher-potential carrier such as PEP would be a superior "universal energy currency" for the cell. Which analysis BEST explains why ATP's INTERMEDIATE position, not the top position, is exactly what makes it the cell's universal energy intermediary?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Principles of Bioenergetics.'

The answer is B because a phosphate group flows spontaneously (exergonically) from a higher-transfer-potential compound to a lower-potential one. Since ATP sits in the MIDDLE of the ranking, higher-potential donors such as PEP or creatine phosphate can spontaneously hand their phosphate to ADP, regenerating ATP; and ATP, in turn, sits above acceptors like glucose, so ATP can spontaneously donate its phosphate to form glucose-6-phosphate. Being intermediate is precisely what lets ATP work both directions, receiving from the high end and delivering to the low end, which is the defining behavior of a universal intermediary.

(Choice B) is correct: it captures the two-way relay role that follows directly from ATP occupying a position below the top donors and above the bottom acceptors.

(Choice A) is incorrect: ATP is explicitly intermediate, not at the top. A top-ranked carrier could donate but could not be easily recharged by transfer from anything higher, which would make it a poor renewable currency.

(Choice C) is incorrect: PEP's high potential does not freeze it. PEP readily DONATES its phosphate (that is what a high potential means). The claim that PEP cannot participate reverses the meaning of the ranking.

(Choice D) is incorrect: ATP is not the highest-energy phosphate in the ranking (PEP and creatine phosphate are above it), and the value of ATP is its intermediacy, not maximal stored energy. This restates the very misconception the stem sets out to overturn.

This item rewards Skill 2 reasoning: applying the rule that phosphate flows down a transfer-potential gradient to deduce why a mid-ranked carrier can both accept and donate, and is therefore the ideal universal intermediary.$px$, options = $px$[{"label":"A","text":"ATP sits at the top of the ranking, so it can donate a phosphate to any acceptor while never needing to be recharged by another molecule."},{"label":"B","text":"Because ATP is intermediate, higher-ranked donors like PEP can spontaneously re-form it, and ATP can spontaneously phosphorylate lower-ranked acceptors like glucose-a two-way relay."},{"label":"C","text":"Because PEP has the highest transfer potential, it can neither accept nor donate phosphate under cellular conditions, which is why the cell must fall back on ATP."},{"label":"D","text":"ATP's phosphoanhydride bonds hold more total energy than any other phosphate compound in the cell, so all transfers must flow outward from ATP only."}]$px$::jsonb WHERE id = 'd370b658-e1bb-458a-a728-34c8727ec96a';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.' The dominance pattern of a single gene is diagnosed from how the heterozygote expresses the two alleles' products: codominance means both alleles are simultaneously and fully expressed in the heterozygote, so each parental product appears at its full homozygous level, whereas incomplete dominance produces an intermediate result in which the heterozygote falls between the two homozygotes. The key measurement here is quantitative: each pigment in the F1 reaches ~100 units, the same level it reaches in its own homozygous parent, so neither product is diluted or reduced. (Choice A) is correct: because the F1 makes both the crimson-allele product and the ivory-allele product at their full ~100-unit homozygous levels rather than at reduced levels, both alleles are being expressed independently and completely in the heterozygote, which is the defining molecular signature of codominance (as in the MN blood groups, where a heterozygote displays both the M and N antigens fully). (Choice B) is incorrect: incomplete dominance predicts an intermediate outcome. For a gene controlling one measurable pigment, the heterozygote would show a reduced or blended level (for example, roughly 50 units of a single pigment, the analog of a pink snapdragon), not each of two pigments at its full 100-unit level; the data show full, not intermediate, expression, so this pattern is ruled out. (Choice C) is incorrect: under complete dominance one allele's product would be expressed and the other silenced, so the F1 would show only crimson (~100 units) or only ivory, whereas both products are detected at full level. (Choice D) is incorrect: epistasis is an interaction between two different genes in which one gene masks the effect of another, but this scenario involves the two alleles of a single gene and shows no masking of either product, so a two-gene interaction cannot account for the measurements. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the quantitative definitions of the dominance patterns to expression-level data and infer that full, independent expression of both allele products, rather than a reduced or intermediate level, identifies codominance.$px$ WHERE id = 'd383941b-5c82-405f-9316-b61bd2cf15ff';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is C because the all-single-bond structure keeps every formal charge at zero while the double-bond "fix" separates charge without ever restoring an octet. Step 1 (electron bookkeeping): \(\text{Xe}\) contributes 8 and four \(\text{F}\) contribute \(4 \times 7 = 28\), for 36 total electrons (18 pairs); the four \(\text{Xe}-\text{F}\) single bonds use 4 pairs and the four fluorine octets use \(4 \times 3 = 12\) pairs, accounting for 16 pairs (32 electrons), so the remaining 2 pairs (4 electrons) localize on \(\text{Xe}\). Step 2 (expanded octet): \(\text{Xe}\) then carries 4 bonding pairs plus 2 lone pairs, i.e. 12 electrons, an expanded octet that period-5 \(\text{Xe}\) can accommodate. Step 3 (formal charge of the single-bond form): \(\text{FC}_{\text{Xe}} = 8 - 4 - \tfrac{1}{2}(8) = 0\) and each \(\text{F}\) gives \(7 - 6 - \tfrac{1}{2}(2) = 0\), so every formal charge is zero. Step 4 (the double-bond "fix"): to make one \(\text{Xe}=\text{F}\) double bond while conserving all 36 electrons, that fluorine must donate one of its lone pairs into the new bond, leaving \(\text{Xe}\) with 3 single bonds, 1 double bond, and 2 lone pairs, i.e. \(10 + 4 = 14\) electrons, not an octet but an even larger expanded shell. Now \(\text{FC}_{\text{Xe}} = 8 - 4 - \tfrac{1}{2}(10) = -1\) and the double-bonded \(\text{F}\) becomes \(7 - 4 - \tfrac{1}{2}(4) = +1\) (the three single-bonded \(\text{F}\) remain \(0\)), so the charges sum to \(0\) as required for a neutral molecule but are no longer all zero. Because the single-bond structure achieves all-zero formal charges and the double-bond structure forces a \(-1/+1\) separation while still failing to give xenon an octet, the original structure is preferred. (Choice A) This undercounts the leftover by one pair: \(36 - 32 = 4\) electrons remain (two lone pairs, 12 electrons on \(\text{Xe}\)), not one lone pair, and the correct formal charge on \(\text{Xe}\) in the single-bond form is \(0\), not \(+2\). (Choice B) The proposed double bond does not restore an octet on xenon, which actually rises to 14 electrons, and it creates a \(-1/+1\) formal-charge separation rather than the all-zero set claimed, so it cannot make the all-single-bond structure inferior. (Choice D) Xenon does not retain a plain octet in either structure; the 4 electrons that have nowhere else to go force an expanded octet of 12 in the single-bond form and of 14 once a double bond is made, and that conversion changes the formal charges from all-zero to a \(-1/+1\) pair. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain a total-electron count, an expanded-octet determination, and a charge-conserving formal-charge comparison to judge which Lewis structure is governed by the lowest formal charges.$px$ WHERE id = 'd3ed9301-3e41-47a7-9018-9a8b2f467f0a';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is A because the D/L designation is an absolute-configuration label, not a measurement of optical behavior. For a sugar, the D or L prefix is fixed by the configuration at the highest-numbered (most carbonyl-remote) chiral carbon, compared to D- or L-glyceraldehyde: if that reference center matches D-glyceraldehyde, the sugar is D. The direction a solution rotates plane-polarized light, clockwise (dextrorotatory, +) or counterclockwise (levorotatory, −), is an independent, experimentally measured property that depends on the whole molecule and cannot be predicted from the D/L prefix. A D-sugar can therefore be levorotatory (D-fructose is a classic example), so a leftward rotation from a confirmed D-sugar is entirely expected, not a malfunction.

(Choice A) is correct: it correctly separates the two ideas. D is set by the far chiral center's configuration and says nothing about rotation direction, which is exactly why no contradiction exists.

(Choice B) is incorrect: it repeats the core misconception that "D" implies dextrorotatory. The prefix reflects spatial configuration relative to glyceraldehyde, not sign of rotation, so a D-sugar is free to be levorotatory and the measurement need not be in error.

(Choice C) is incorrect: it reaches the right verdict (student is wrong) for the wrong structural reason. The D/L reference center is the chiral carbon farthest from the carbonyl, not the carbonyl carbon itself (the carbonyl carbon of an aldose is not even a chiral center), so the stated basis is false.

(Choice D) is incorrect: it assumes rotation direction reveals D versus L. Because sign of rotation is not tied to the D/L configuration, a counterclockwise reading does not force the molecule to be the L enantiomer; the structural analysis identifying it as D stands.

This question requires Skill 2 reasoning: rather than restating a definition, you must apply the distinction between absolute configuration and optical rotation to a described measurement, predict what the D prefix does and does not tell you, and reject a plausible but false inference.$px$ WHERE id = 'd4029c66-a702-44e7-bd17-e78470d225ce';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure.' Elements are sorted into three classes by their physical behavior: metals are shiny, malleable, and excellent conductors of heat and electricity; nonmetals are dull, brittle, and poor conductors; and metalloids conduct only moderately and share some properties of each, so they occupy the stepped diagonal 'staircase' with metals to its left and nonmetals to its right. A defining fingerprint of a metalloid (a semiconductor such as Si, Ge, or As) is an intermediate conductivity that increases with temperature, unlike a true metal, whose conductivity falls slightly as it warms. (Choice A) is incorrect: Element W is lustrous, malleable, and conducts as well as copper, making it a metal that sits well to the left of the staircase, not on it. (Choice B) is incorrect: Element X is dull, brittle, an insulator (~10⁻¹⁰ S/m), and forms an acidic oxide. These are nonmetal traits placing it to the right of the staircase. (Choice C) is correct: Element Y has a metallic sheen yet is brittle, conducts only moderately (~10³ S/m), and that conductivity rises when warmed and enables transistor behavior. That is the semiconductor signature of a metalloid, the class that lies along the staircase itself. (Choice D) is incorrect: Element Z is soft, shiny, highly conductive, and reacts with water to form a base, which are the hallmarks of a reactive metal (an alkali or alkaline earth metal) far to the left of the staircase, not on it. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the property criteria that define metals, metalloids, and nonmetals to quantitative and behavioral data and then translate that classification into a position relative to the metalloid staircase.$px$ WHERE id = 'd40dc1c9-79ed-4c07-8cbd-9a4f25dbdced';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This item tests the quantum-number constraint linking the principal quantum number \(n\) to the allowed azimuthal values \(l\) (electronic structure / quantum model). The number of permitted subshell types in a shell equals \(n\), since \(l\) runs over the integers \(0, 1, 2, \ldots, n-1\). Seven distinct subshell types therefore require \(n = 7\), giving \(l = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\). The subshells with \(l \ge 3\) are \(l = 3, 4, 5, 6\), four values in all, so the answer is \(4\).\n(Choice B) The value \(3\) counts only \(l = 4, 5, 6\), excluding \(l = 3\) itself; the condition \(l \ge 3\) is inclusive of the boundary \(l = 3\).\n(Choice C) The value \(6\) is the largest allowed \(l\) (equal to \(n-1\)) rather than a count of subshells meeting the threshold, and it would correspond to counting all subshells with \(l \ge 1\).\n(Choice D) The value \(7\) is the total number of subshells in the shell (equal to \(n\)), not the subset restricted by \(l \ge 3\).\nSkill 2$px$ WHERE id = 'd42aa39f-cf77-4f83-8cc3-c972f21dd757';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A researcher is designing a metalloenzyme active site that requires one amino acid residue whose side chain can readily shuttle a proton, existing in an appreciable mix of both its protonated and deprotonated forms, under the near-neutral conditions of the cytoplasm (pH ≈ 7.4). She consults a table of side-chain pKa values: aspartate ≈ 3.9, histidine ≈ 6.5, lysine ≈ 10.5, and arginine ≈ 12.5. Which residue best meets her requirement?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' Whether a side chain is protonated at a given pH depends on how its pKa compares with that pH: by the +2 rule, a group is roughly half protonated when \(\text{pH} = \text{pK}_\text{a}\), and it shifts to ~99% one form once the pH is about two units away from its pKa. A residue can act as a proton shuttle (general acid-base) only if a meaningful fraction of it sits in each ionization state at the working pH, which requires a side-chain pKa close to that pH. (Choice B) is correct: histidine's imidazole side chain has a pKa of about 6.5, only ~0.9 units below physiological pH, so at pH 7.4 it exists as an appreciable mixture of protonated and deprotonated forms and can readily gain or donate a proton. That is the reason histidine is the residue most often found doing general acid-base chemistry in enzyme active sites. (Choice A) is incorrect: aspartate's carboxyl side chain has a pKa near 3.9, roughly 3.5 units below pH 7.4, so it is essentially fully deprotonated (negatively charged) and cannot shuttle a proton at physiological pH. (Choice C) is incorrect: lysine's ε-amino side chain has a pKa of about 10.5, roughly 3 units above pH 7.4, so it remains essentially fully protonated (positively charged) and does not readily give up its proton under these conditions. (Choice D) is incorrect: arginine's guanidino side chain has a pKa of about 12.5 and is the most basic standard residue, but that high pKa means it stays fully protonated at physiological pH. Being the "most basic" makes it a poorer, not better, proton shuttle at pH 7.4. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between side-chain pKa and ambient pH to predict which residue is significantly ionizable at physiological pH.$px$ WHERE id = 'd458540f-9425-4e83-b1d7-3bcfed4e05b7';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A pediatric endocrinologist administers a very small dose of estrogen to a healthy 6-year-old and measures circulating GnRH, LH, and FSH afterward. All three hormones fall to strikingly low levels, a far steeper suppression than the same dose would produce in an adult. Which mechanism best accounts for this exaggerated drop?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Reproductive Endocrinology (BIO)'. The answer is C because in prepubertal children the negative-feedback sensitivity of the hypothalamus and pituitary is very high, so even very low concentrations of sex steroids feed back strongly to keep GnRH, LH, and FSH production low. In pre-pubertal children this negative-feedback system is extremely sensitive, meaning that even minute concentrations of androgens or estrogens suppress the hypothalamus and pituitary and hold GnRH, LH, and FSH production low. The transition into puberty requires this feedback sensitivity to decline, so that progressively larger concentrations of sex steroid hormones become necessary to shut down LH and FSH production. A child's axis therefore responds to a tiny steroid dose with the steep suppression observed here, whereas an adult's desensitized axis would not. (Choice A) Increased gonadal sensitivity to FSH and LH is a separate pubertal change that makes the gonads more responsive to gonadotropins; it acts downstream and would not drive feedback suppression of the central axis. (Choice B) GnRH secretion is restrained, not absent, before puberty (nocturnal pulsatile LH release is already detectable years before physical changes), so the pulse generator is not "completely silent," and feedback sensitivity is the operative reason. (Choice C) High central negative-feedback sensitivity to low sex-steroid levels is exactly the established prepubertal mechanism, so this is correct. (Choice D) The prepubertal anterior pituitary does produce gonadotropins (detectable LH appears around age eight or nine, before puberty), so the claim that no gonadotropins exist is false. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires inferring from an experimental result (exaggerated gonadotropin suppression after a low steroid dose) which underlying property of the immature reproductive axis explains the data: feedback sensitivity.$px$ WHERE id = 'd45d351d-907e-4f72-8541-8850e469bec3';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Titration (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because in a potentiometric titration the platinum indicator electrode senses the solution's redox potential, which by the Nernst equation depends on the logarithm of the ratio of oxidized to reduced species (for the iron couple, \(E = E^\circ_{\text{Fe}^{3+}/\text{Fe}^{2+}} - \tfrac{RT}{F}\ln\frac{[\text{Fe}^{2+}]}{[\text{Fe}^{3+}]}\)). Before the equivalence point this ratio changes only gradually as \(\text{Ce}^{4+}\) oxidizes \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\) to \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\), so the potential drifts slowly. At the equivalence volume the last trace of the limiting species is consumed, the controlling couple switches from \(\text{Fe}^{3+}/\text{Fe}^{2+}\) to \(\text{Ce}^{4+}/\text{Ce}^{3+}\), and one small drop of titrant swings the relevant concentration ratio by orders of magnitude. Because the potential depends on the logarithm of that ratio, this produces a large, abrupt jump in voltage: the steep inflection in the potential-versus-volume curve that locates the endpoint without any chemical indicator. (Choice A) The cell potential need not pass through \(0\ \text{V}\) at the endpoint (its value is set by the standard potentials of the two couples and is generally nonzero), and equal amounts of oxidized and reduced species correspond to the half-equivalence point of a couple, not to the equivalence point of the titration. (Choice B) A constant nonzero potential is what is seen during the gradual pre-equivalence buffering region, not at the endpoint; the endpoint is marked by the sudden change in potential, not by a plateau. (Choice D) The slope of the potential-versus-volume curve is smallest, not largest, at the start; the maximum slope occurs at the equivalence point, so a linear rise that is steepest at the beginning misidentifies where the endpoint signal appears. Skill 2$px$ WHERE id = 'd47c880a-d241-441e-af1c-e8e08d68993e';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is C because an enzyme accelerates a reaction by binding the transition state more tightly than the free substrate, which lowers the energy of the transition state and therefore lowers the activation energy \(E_a\). Pauling postulated that "the only thing that a catalyst must do is bind the transition state more tightly than the substrate," and stabilizing the transition state is what catalyzes the reaction. Because the enzyme only changes the rate at which equilibrium is reached, the thermodynamics, \(\Delta G\) and \(K_\text{eq}\), are unchanged, exactly as observed. (Choice A) is incorrect: tightly binding the free substrate would deepen the starting energy well and effectively increase \(E_a\) rather than lower it; the enzyme must bind the transition state MORE tightly than the substrate, not the reverse. (Choice B) is incorrect: stabilizing the product lowers the product's energy and would shift the equilibrium position, yet enzymes leave \(\Delta G\) and \(K_\text{eq}\) unchanged, so product stabilization is not the catalytic mechanism. (Choice C) is correct: binding the transition state more tightly than the substrate lowers its energy, reduces \(E_a\), and speeds the reaction without altering \(\Delta G\) or \(K_\text{eq}\). (Choice D) is incorrect: this reverses the mechanism. Raising the transition-state energy would increase \(E_a\) and slow the reaction, whereas catalysts LOWER the energy of the transition state. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that enzymes lower activation energy by stabilizing the transition state while leaving reaction thermodynamics unchanged.$px$ WHERE id = 'd482f246-b8c8-4aad-9966-5e1e605e73c3';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.' A trait shared by two lineages is homologous only if it was inherited from their common ancestor; if the same trait instead evolved separately in each lineage after they diverged, it is analogous and its appearance is the product of convergent evolution. The decisive clue here is that the most recent common ancestor of birds and mammals did NOT have a four-chambered heart, so each group must have built that heart on its own. The four-chambered hearts of birds and mammals arose independently from an ancestral three-chambered heart, and the independent evolution of the same or a similar biological trait is what defines convergent evolution. (Choice A) is incorrect: divergent evolution describes lineages that evolve in different directions from a shared starting trait, and homology requires that the four-chambered heart came from the common ancestor, but the evidence states the ancestor lacked it, so the structure is neither homologous nor a case of divergence. (Choice B) is incorrect: vestigial structures are reduced, largely nonfunctional remnants of a feature present in an ancestor, whereas the four-chambered heart is fully functional and, per the evidence, was not present in the ancestor at all. (Choice C) is correct: because the shared ancestor lacked the four-chambered heart, birds and mammals each evolved it separately, making the hearts analogous structures generated by convergent evolution. (Choice D) is incorrect: it correctly identifies convergent evolution but pairs it with the wrong structure type. Traits arising from independent evolution are analogous, not homologous, since homology by definition requires inheritance from a common ancestor. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that homology requires inheritance from a common ancestor while analogy arises from convergent evolution, and use the ancestral-state evidence to classify the trait correctly.$px$ WHERE id = 'd4902cdf-067a-4b9c-a5b8-3674a04b7c2c';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.' Extensions to Mendelian inheritance are distinguished by how the heterozygote expresses its two alleles: codominance is the simultaneous, full expression of both alleles, whereas incomplete dominance produces a single intermediate phenotype that resembles neither homozygous parent. The key data here are that the heterozygote makes both parental gene products, each at its full homozygous amount, and displays both colors as distinct stripes. (Choice A) is correct: the heterozygote expresses both the yellow and the black pigment proteins simultaneously and fully, so both parental traits appear together rather than blending. This is the defining signature of codominance, exactly analogous to a heterozygote for the MN blood group expressing both the M and N antigens on its red blood cells. (Choice B) is incorrect: incomplete dominance would yield a single intermediate phenotype (for example, uniformly olive or dull green feathers) reflecting a blended or reduced output, not the full, separate expression of both distinct pigment proteins detected here. (Choice C) is incorrect: complete dominance of the black allele would mask the yellow allele entirely, so the heterozygote would appear solid black and no yellow pigment protein would be detectable, contradicting the assay result. (Choice D) is incorrect: a dosage effect would leave one product expressed at a reduced level in the heterozygote, but the assay shows each protein at its full homozygous amount, so no allele is being under-expressed. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definitions of codominance and incomplete dominance to biochemical and phenotypic evidence about the heterozygote in order to infer the underlying inheritance pattern.$px$ WHERE id = 'd4d44718-4579-4f88-bb34-2de9641930f9';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A proteomics lab is analyzing a cell lysate. On a standard 1D SDS-PAGE gel, two proteins of interest, Protein X and Protein Y, both migrate as a single overlapping band at an apparent molecular weight of 45 kDa. Suspecting the band contains two distinct species, a researcher measures the isoelectric points of the purified proteins and finds pI = 5.2 for Protein X and pI = 8.6 for Protein Y. The lysate is then re-run using two-dimensional gel electrophoresis, in which isoelectric focusing across a pH 3 to 11 gradient precedes the SDS-PAGE step. Which of the following best describes the expected result for Protein X and Protein Y on the 2D gel, and the reason for it?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Separations and Purifications.'

The answer is A because the two proteins co-migrate on 1D SDS-PAGE for a single reason: SDS coats proteins with uniform negative charge so that they separate essentially by molecular weight alone, and both are ~45 kDa. Two-dimensional electrophoresis adds a first dimension, isoelectric focusing (IEF), that separates on an entirely independent property: the isoelectric point. In IEF a protein migrates through a pH gradient until it reaches the pH equal to its pI, where its net charge is zero and it stops. Because Protein X (pI 5.2) and Protein Y (pI 8.6) focus at very different points in the pH 3 to 11 gradient, they are placed at different horizontal positions before SDS-PAGE ever runs. The two dimensions are orthogonal (pI, then mass), so proteins that are identical in one property but differ in the other are pulled apart. Since X and Y differ in pI, they resolve into two separate spots.

(Choice A) is correct: 2D electrophoresis resolves the pair precisely because the added IEF dimension sorts by pI, a property on which X and Y differ, positioning them horizontally before the size step, whereas 1D SDS-PAGE only sorted by the mass they share.

(Choice B) is incorrect: the SDS-PAGE dimension separates by molecular weight, and both proteins are ~45 kDa. That dimension already ran in 1D and failed to split them; adding it again in the second dimension cannot resolve species of equal mass. The resolving power comes from the new pI dimension, not the size dimension.

(Choice C) is incorrect: the two dimensions are not redundant. SDS-PAGE separates by molecular weight (SDS masks intrinsic charge to give uniform charge-to-mass ratio), while IEF separates by pI. Only IEF is charge-related, so it is false that both dimensions separate on net charge; if they did, 2D would add no resolving power over 1D.

(Choice D) is incorrect: in IEF a protein does not stop at an electrode. It migrates until it reaches the position where the gradient pH equals its pI and its net charge becomes zero; a species below its pI is positively charged and moves toward the cathode, not toward the anode. Because X and Y have different pIs, they halt at different focusing positions, which is exactly what allows them to be resolved.

This item is Skill 2 because it does not ask for the definition of 2D electrophoresis; it requires applying the principle that the two dimensions probe independent properties to predict, from given pI and mass data, that proteins sharing mass but differing in pI will be separated by the orthogonal IEF step.$px$ WHERE id = 'd5490507-c75b-4c68-b0f1-8be0f3d73fb0';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is D because the observations (a change in seconds, no change in enzyme amount, and rapid reversibility) point to covalent modification of pre-existing enzymes. Insulin acts through a receptor tyrosine kinase and glucagon through a GPCR/cAMP cascade, and both converge on adding or removing phosphate groups. Because phosphate can be put on and taken off, the same enzyme is toggled between active and inactive states almost instantly.

(Choice A) is incorrect: making new enzyme by transcription and translation takes hours and would change the amount of enzyme, contradicting the fast, amount-neutral switching described.
(Choice B) is incorrect: glucagon and insulin are water-soluble peptides that do not cross the membrane; they act through surface receptors, not by entering and binding enzymes directly. The true allosteric effectors of these enzymes are small metabolites, not the hormones themselves.
(Choice D) is correct: reversible phosphorylation/dephosphorylation of enzymes already present allows activity to be flipped rapidly and undone just as fast, exactly matching the described behavior.
(Choice C) is incorrect: degrading enzymes would be slow and effectively irreversible until resynthesis, the opposite of the rapid, reversible switching observed.

This question tests Skill 2 (Scientific Reasoning and Problem-Solving) because it asks you to infer the underlying signaling mechanism from features of the response (speed, reversibility, constant enzyme amount) rather than to restate how a receptor works.$px$ WHERE id = 'd56289be-5136-42f2-8b6c-a530ded1ddcc';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' A heterotropic allosteric activator binds an allosteric (non-active) site and stabilizes the enzyme's active R conformation, redistributing the T/R equilibrium toward the active R state. Because the R state has higher apparent affinity for substrate, the activator raises the rate at sub-saturating substrate and lowers the substrate concentration needed for half-maximal rate, shifting the sigmoidal curve to the left. That is the mirror image of how inhibitory effectors on hemoglobin shift the binding curve to the right, lowering the apparent affinity. (Choice A) is correct: stabilizing the R state increases apparent substrate affinity, so the half-maximal point occurs at a lower substrate concentration and the curve moves left. (Choice B) is incorrect: shifting the curve to the right (higher half-maximal substrate, lower apparent affinity) is the effect of an allosteric inhibitor, not an activator. This reverses the direction of the modulation. (Choice C) is incorrect: this confuses a K-type effect with a V-type effect; a heterotropic activator of this enzyme changes apparent substrate affinity (the position of the curve), not the maximal catalytic rate reached at saturating substrate. (Choice D) is incorrect: an activator does not simply abolish cooperativity while leaving the midpoint fixed; stabilizing the R state shifts the equilibrium and moves the curve's position, so the half-maximal substrate concentration decreases rather than staying the same. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the R/T equilibrium model to predict how stabilizing the active conformation repositions a sigmoidal rate-versus-substrate curve.$px$ WHERE id = 'd56cd755-9cb1-4532-81bc-a44e058752d3';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Early Embryonic Development.' Consider the first trial's outcome: removing one blastomere leaves the embryo with missing structures or unable to develop. That result is the diagnostic signature of determinate (mosaic) cleavage, in which the developmental fate of each blastomere is already fixed and no other cell can take over its assigned contribution. The text states that in determinate cleavage "the developmental fate of each embryonic cell is already determined" and "a given cell does not have the ability to develop into any cell type other than its original destination," so "removal of a blastomere from an embryo with determinate cleavage can result in missing structures, and embryos that fail to develop." Because species X shows this signature, its early cells are already committed; twinning and compensation are properties the text assigns instead to indeterminate cleavage, so they cannot occur here. (Choice A) is correct: since each blastomere is already committed to only part of the body plan, an isolated blastomere carries only its share of the developmental program, so neither separated cell can build the whole animal. That matches the deficient outcome seen when a blastomere is removed. (Choice B) is incorrect: separated blastomeres forming identical twins is a hallmark of indeterminate cleavage, where the text says "twins (clones) can be produced" from separated blastomeres because the cells are "not yet fully committed"; the first trial rules this out for species X. (Choice C) is incorrect: fate in determinate cleavage is set by committed developmental programs, not by which fragment inherits more cytoplasm, and the text does not describe survival being decided by relative cell size; both cells would be equally deficient rather than one being fully normal. (Choice D) is incorrect: separated blastomeres do not fuse and reconstitute the original embryo; cleavage subdivides the egg into ever-smaller cells with no mechanism described for re-merging to restore the whole, so this outcome is not predicted for either cleavage type. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the diagnostic behavior of determinate cleavage (loss of structures after blastomere removal) to infer the cleavage type and then predict a second, different experimental outcome for that same type.$px$ WHERE id = 'd56ce092-5f7f-4b5c-8895-6a33d813f877';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is A because the defining feature of a permissive hormone is that it need not initiate a response itself, yet its presence is required for another hormone to act fully. With cortisol deficient, glucagon and its receptors are normal, but glucagon cannot produce its full glycemic effect, so the same dose raises blood glucose far less.

(Choice A) is correct: it states the permissive relationship. Cortisol enables glucagon's full effect without necessarily triggering it.
(Choice B) is incorrect: cortisol (a steroid acting on gene transcription) and glucagon (a peptide acting on a surface cAMP receptor) do not share a receptor, so competition for binding sites is not the mechanism; furthermore, competition would free sites and amplify the response, the opposite of what is observed.
(Choice C) is incorrect: if the deficit were merely cortisol's own missing glucose contribution, glucagon's effect would be unchanged; here the measured rise after glucagon is itself diminished, which additive reasoning cannot explain.
(Choice D) is incorrect: cortisol enhances, not restrains, glucagon, so its absence blunts rather than amplifies the response.

This question tests Skill 2 (Scientific Reasoning and Problem-Solving) by asking you to reason from an observed blunted response to the concept of a permissive hormone.$px$ WHERE id = 'd5f24e3f-770f-4359-9d16-3e04b5d50d62';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A biochemist needs to prepare a buffer with a target \(\text{pH} = 5.04\) using a weak acid that has \(\text{p}K_a = 4.74\) and its conjugate base. Using the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation, what approximate ratio of conjugate base to weak acid, \([\text{A}^-]/[\text{HA}]\), must the biochemist establish in the solution?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The Henderson-Hasselbalch equation states that \(\text{pH} = \text{p}K_a + \log\!\left(\frac{[\text{A}^-]}{[\text{HA}]}\right)\). Rearranging to solve for the ratio gives \(\log\!\left(\frac{[\text{A}^-]}{[\text{HA}]}\right) = \text{pH} - \text{p}K_a\), so \(\frac{[\text{A}^-]}{[\text{HA}]} = 10^{(\text{pH} - \text{p}K_a)}\). The answer to this question is D because \(\text{pH} - \text{p}K_a = 5.04 - 4.74 = 0.30\), and \(10^{0.30} \approx 2.0\); the conjugate base must be present at roughly twice the concentration of the weak acid (about \(2:1\)) to push the pH 0.30 unit above the \(\text{p}K_a\). (Choice A) A ratio of \(10\) corresponds to \(10^{1}\), which would require the pH to lie a full unit (1.00) above the \(\text{p}K_a\), not the 0.30 unit that the actual target pH demands. (Choice B) A \(1:1\) ratio gives \(\log(1) = 0\), so the pH would equal the \(\text{p}K_a\) of 4.74; this choice ignores the required \(+0.30\) offset between the target pH and the \(\text{p}K_a\). (Choice C) A ratio of \(0.5\) corresponds to \(10^{-0.30}\), which would arise from computing \(\text{p}K_a - \text{pH}\) instead of \(\text{pH} - \text{p}K_a\), inverting the ratio and yielding a pH below the \(\text{p}K_a\) rather than above it. Skill 4$px$ WHERE id = 'd624f17e-c80b-4dcb-a2b2-4a8e1156743b';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is C because at rest, drawing air into the lungs is the active phase: the diaphragm and external intercostal muscles contract, enlarging the thoracic cavity, which lowers intrapulmonary pressure below atmospheric pressure so air flows in. Quiet release of air, by contrast, is passive. It results from elastic recoil of the lungs and chest wall when those muscles relax, requiring no muscular work. (Choice C) is correct: contraction of the diaphragm and external intercostals is precisely what makes quiet inhalation an active process. (Choice A) is incorrect: at rest, releasing air is passive and driven by elastic recoil; abdominal muscles assist only during forced (active) exhalation, not quiet breathing. (Choice B) is incorrect: the two phases are not symmetric. Quiet inhalation is active while quiet exhalation is passive, so they do not require equal muscular effort. (Choice D) is incorrect: bulk air movement into and out of the lungs is driven by pressure differences created by muscle-driven volume changes, not by diffusion; diffusion governs gas exchange across the alveolar membrane, not the movement of air through the airways. Skill: This item tests the ability to identify which phase of the breathing cycle depends on active muscular contraction versus passive elastic recoil.$px$ WHERE id = 'd6fc66d9-2f70-446e-8350-0de669037c41';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is D because the size of the lung is set by the transpulmonary pressure, the difference between intra-alveolar pressure and intrapleural pressure. A maximal effort that lifts the rib cage further than quiet breathing pulls the chest wall outward and makes intrapleural pressure more negative, widening the transpulmonary difference and expanding the lung more; opening the chest wall lets outside air enter the pleural space, so intrapleural pressure rises toward atmospheric, the transpulmonary difference collapses, and the lung's own elastic recoil draws it partially inward. (Choice D) is correct: greater rib-cage elevation deepens the negative intrapleural pressure and increases expansion, while the breach equalizes intrapleural pressure with the atmosphere and lets elastic recoil pull the lung inward. (Choice A) is incorrect: it reverses the effort effect (a larger inspiratory effort increases, not decreases, expansion), and it wrongly claims the lung expands outward after the breach, when losing the negative pleural gradient instead causes collapse. (Choice B) is incorrect: the first clause is right, but a breach cannot drive the lung to expand further outward; admitting air to the pleural space does not push the lung open, and without a negative intrapleural pressure holding it against the chest wall, elastic recoil dominates and the lung recedes. (Choice C) is incorrect: raising the rib cage beyond the quiet-breathing level must increase expansion rather than leave lung volume unchanged, even though it correctly recognizes that the breach causes inward collapse. This item exercises reasoning about the mechanical basis of a living system by predicting lung volume changes from shifts in the intrapleural pressure gradient across two conditions.$px$ WHERE id = 'd7005733-9328-441f-a8ec-a4a0ad4782af';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is C because of the diagnostic behavior described: a cofactor that binds, is reduced as the substrate is oxidized, then dissociates as a product and is regenerated elsewhere in the cell rather than on the enzyme. That is the hallmark of a loosely bound coenzyme, one that binds only weakly to the enzyme, is released from the active site, and functions as a substrate for the reaction. \(\text{NAD}^+\) and \(\text{NADP}^+\) are exactly this case: they act as dissociable substrates/cofactors, and the free enzyme stays continually active because the \(\text{NAD}^+\) cofactor binds (as a substrate) and dissociates (as a product) after each catalytic cycle. (Choice A) is wrong because thiamine pyrophosphate is covalently attached to the enzyme, as in pyruvate dehydrogenase, so it never dissociates as a freely diffusing product. (Choice B) is a partial-truth trap: FAD is also regenerated each turnover, but it is tightly or covalently attached to an enzyme, so it must be regenerated during each catalytic cycle. It is re-oxidized in place on the enzyme and does not diffuse away as a product, which is the very feature the stem requires. (Choice C) is correct: a small organic cofactor that binds as a substrate, is reduced, and dissociates as a product to be re-oxidized elsewhere is a loosely bound coenzyme, and \(\text{NAD}^+\) is the prototypical dissociable, continually recycled example. (Choice D) is wrong because heme is a tightly bound prosthetic group (tight-binding coenzymes are called prosthetic groups, and heme is held in place by interactions with histidine residues), so it stays coordinated to the enzyme rather than diffusing off as a product. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinction between loosely bound (dissociable) coenzymes and tightly bound prosthetic groups to a described catalytic cycle and identify the cofactor whose binding behavior matches the dissociate-and-recycle pattern.$px$ WHERE id = 'd7372fbe-d086-4045-9f8c-a72f19a0d655';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' Functional classification of joints is set by the amount of mobility between the articulating bones, not by the joint's structural material: the same structural class can map to different functional classes, and both fibrous and cartilaginous joints may be functionally classified as a synarthrosis (an immobile joint). Two protective joints belong together in the synarthrosis category: the cranial sutures, the fibrous joints between the skull bones that surround and protect the brain, and the manubriosternal joint, the cartilaginous joint that unites the manubrium and body of the sternum to protect the heart. Each forms a strong, immobile union that shields an internal organ. (Choice A) is incorrect because a diarthrosis is a freely moveable joint comprising the synovial joints of the limbs; neither a skull suture nor the manubriosternal joint permits free movement, so this is the wrong mobility extreme. (Choice B) is incorrect because an amphiarthrosis is a slightly moveable joint such as the fibrocartilaginous intervertebral discs and pubic symphysis, which allow limited motion; the two protective joints described here form immobile unions rather than slightly mobile ones. (Choice C) is correct: both joints form strong, immobile unions that protect an internal organ, and an immobile joint is a synarthrosis; because functional class is assigned by mobility alone, a fibrous suture and a cartilaginous manubriosternal joint can share this category even though their structures differ. (Choice D) is incorrect because it conflates structural and functional classification; both fibrous and cartilaginous joints can be functionally classified as synarthroses, so differing structural material does not prevent two joints from sharing one functional category. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that functional joint classes are defined by mobility, that immobile protective joints are synarthroses, and that structurally different joints can share a functional category.$px$ WHERE id = 'd77d490c-d5d7-46bd-ae5f-e139ec7c9df0';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is C because the probe marks the basal face (the side held to the basement membrane), so the brush border of absorptive microvilli must be on the opposite, apical face (the surface exposed to the intestinal lumen). Epithelial cells are polarized, exhibiting structural and functional differences between the exposed apical surface and the basal surface that lies close to underlying body structures; the basal lamina provides an attachment site for the epithelium, separating it from the underlying connective tissue and forming the basement membrane. Because the probe binds the hemidesmosome integrins that link cells to the extracellular matrix such as the basal lamina, it labels the basal face. The absorptive microvilli are cylindrical apical extensions of the plasma membrane of the mucosal epithelial cells, whose combined mass of bristles is termed the brush border and which greatly enhance absorption. They therefore sit on the apical face, opposite the labeled basal side, facing the lumen from which nutrients are absorbed. (Choice A) Reversed: the brush border absorbs nutrients at the apical surface and does not anchor the cell; anchoring to the basement membrane is done by basal hemidesmosomes, the very structures the probe labels, so the brush border is on the opposite face. (Choice B) The lateral surfaces carry junctions, but microvilli and cilia sit on the apical surface, not laterally; tight junctions separate the cells into apical and basal compartments rather than housing the brush border. (Choice C) Correct: the probe-labeled basal face anchors to the basement membrane, so the apical, lumen-facing surface bears the absorptive microvilli. (Choice D) Partial truth. It correctly puts the brush border opposite the probe, but it reverses the orientation: the face away from the basement membrane is the apical surface facing the lumen, not a second surface facing the underlying connective tissue and blood supply (that is the basal side the probe already marks). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must combine the polarity rule with the identity of the labeled marker to predict where the absorptive specialization lies.$px$, options = $px$[{"text":"same face the probe labels, because the brush border is the structure that anchors each cell to the basement membrane.","label":"A"},{"text":"lateral surfaces between adjacent cells, where tight junctions seal the intercellular space and divide the cell into compartments.","label":"B"},{"text":"opposite face from the one the probe labels, the apical surface facing the intestinal lumen.","label":"C"},{"text":"opposite face from the one the probe labels, oriented toward the underlying connective tissue and its blood supply.","label":"D"}]$px$::jsonb WHERE id = 'd7fba913-42b4-4845-bf60-d717e6f83c49';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' Zymogen activation works by proteolytic cleavage. The enzyme loses its first 15 amino acids by selective proteolysis, a hydrolysis that severs a peptide bond and discards a fragment, and the cell has no machinery here to re-form that bond, so it cannot be undone. Phosphorylation is a covalent modification installed by kinases and removed by phosphatases, so a phosphatase can hydrolyze the phosphate ester off the serine and reset that enzyme. You must reason from the underlying chemistry: cutting out a piece of the polypeptide is a one-way change, whereas adding then removing a phosphate group is a reversible cycle. (Choice A) is correct: the phosphatase hydrolyzes the serine phosphate off Enzyme 2 and returns it to its inactive dephosphorylated state, but no cellular process can rejoin the cleaved peptide bond or replace the excised N-terminal fragment of Enzyme 1, so Enzyme 1 stays active. (Choice B) is incorrect: adding more upstream protease cannot ligate the fragment back on. A protease hydrolyzes peptide bonds, it does not form them, so more protease would at most cleave further, never restore the precursor. (Choice C) is incorrect: it wrongly treats both 'off' catalysts as reversing the activating step; the phosphatase does reverse phosphorylation, but the upstream protease does not reverse cleavage because bond hydrolysis discards a fragment that cannot be re-added. (Choice D) is incorrect: 'covalent' does not mean 'permanent.' The phosphoester bond is a covalent modification that a phosphatase readily removes, so Enzyme 2 can indeed be inactivated. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinction between bond-cleaving activation and group-transfer activation to predict which chemical change a cellular catalyst can undo and which it cannot.$px$ WHERE id = 'd86cd542-b722-433a-8c86-590d9ac45b57';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$The body maintains two large glycogen depots: roughly 100 g in the liver and a considerably larger total distributed across skeletal muscle. During an overnight fast, circulating glucose is held within a narrow range so that the brain and red blood cells (tissues that must draw on blood glucose to function) keep working. Considering how each depot is actually used, which statement best captures the division of labor between liver and muscle glycogen that makes this possible?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is C because the two glycogen depots exist to do different jobs. Blood glucose must be held steady for tissues that depend on it, notably the brain and red blood cells, which cannot burn fatty acids. The liver's glycogen is the store that is broken down and released as free glucose to keep that circulating supply topped up between meals. The reason for the split is enzymatic: only the liver (and kidney) possesses glucose-6-phosphatase, the enzyme needed to strip the phosphate from glucose-6-phosphate so free glucose can leave the cell and enter the blood. Skeletal muscle lacks this enzyme, so it cannot export glucose at all; it keeps its glycogen as a private fuel depot, breaking it down to glucose-6-phosphate that stays trapped inside the muscle and is burned in situ to power contraction. That split, with the liver serving the whole body and muscle serving itself, is precisely what lets an overnight fast protect the brain and RBCs.

(Choice C) is correct: liver glycogen is mobilized to sustain blood glucose for glucose-dependent tissues, while muscle glycogen is a dedicated local reserve consumed by the muscle itself, which is the division of labor the stem describes.

(Choice A) is incorrect: it reverses the two roles. Muscle glycogen is not the body's blood-glucose buffer, and liver glycogen is not held back to power muscle contraction; it is the liver, not muscle, that defends circulating glucose for the rest of the body.

(Choice B) is incorrect: muscle glycogen does not feed the bloodstream at all, because muscle lacks glucose-6-phosphatase and cannot release free glucose. The two pools therefore do not both supply blood glucose, and the liver being drawn on first reflects its role as the systemic glucose buffer, not simply its being the smaller depot.

(Choice D) is incorrect: the stores are not interchangeable or shared. Each tissue manages its own glycogen for its own purpose, and only the liver's contribution ever reaches the general circulation as free glucose.

This item tests Skill 2 (scientific reasoning and problem-solving): you reason from the whole-body need to protect glucose-dependent tissues during a fast to infer why the liver and muscle maintain functionally distinct glycogen reserves.$px$ WHERE id = 'd89c8333-0c01-41c9-bd0e-9ec638484340';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is C because during a fast insulin falls and glucagon rises, so tissues divide labor to protect blood glucose for the brain: the liver exports glucose (first by glycogenolysis, then gluconeogenesis), adipose lipolysis supplies free fatty acids and glycerol, and muscle switches to oxidizing those fatty acids while exporting amino acids as gluconeogenic substrate. That spares glucose for the brain.

(Choice A) is incorrect: storing glucose as glycogen, esterifying fatty acids, and GLUT4 uptake for glycogen is the fed, high-insulin anabolic program, not the response to fasting.

(Choice B) is incorrect: it swaps tissue roles. Muscle is not a major triacylglycerol depot and does not supply fatty acids to the liver, and it is muscle (not adipose) that exports alanine to carry nitrogen.

(Choice D) is incorrect: the liver and adipose steps are right, but if muscle kept consuming blood glucose it would compete with the brain; the point of fasting integration is that muscle burns fatty acids to spare glucose.

(Choice C) is correct: it captures the coordinated division of labor (hepatic glucose output, adipose lipolysis, and muscle fatty-acid oxidation with amino-acid export) that sustains blood glucose.

This question tests Skill 2 (Scientific Reasoning and Problem-Solving) by requiring you to integrate three tissues' fasting responses toward one shared goal, protecting the glucose supply.$px$ WHERE id = 'd8c43cf2-a21e-4ef7-be40-d957cb5e9911';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO).' During prometaphase, spindle microtubules from the poles must physically capture the chromosomes. The kinetochore is a protein structure that assembles on the centromere and serves as the point of attachment between the mitotic spindle and the sister chromatids. Microtubules bind the kinetochore, not the centromere DNA it sits on, so losing only the kinetochore removes the chromosome-to-spindle linkage while leaving the poles, the spindle, and the sister-chromatid pairing untouched. (Choice A) is correct: with the kinetochore gone, microtubules have nothing to grip, so chromosomes cannot be tethered or aligned even though the bipolar spindle forms and the chromatids stay paired. That is precisely the phenotype described. (Choice B) is incorrect: the centromere is the DNA region that joins the two sister chromatids and on which the kinetochore assembles; if the centromere itself were lost the sisters would not remain paired, which contradicts the observation that they remain paired. (Choice C) is incorrect: cohesin is the protein that holds sister chromatids together, so inactivating it would cause the chromatids to separate prematurely rather than stay paired, and it does not mediate microtubule attachment. (Choice D) is incorrect: centrioles form the centrosomes from which the spindle emanates; if they were disabled the bipolar spindle would fail to form, yet the scenario states the spindle assembled normally. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinct roles of each mitotic structure to deduce which single loss produces spindle-intact, pairing-intact, attachment-failed chromosomes.$px$ WHERE id = 'd90e5a06-3d3d-4d92-afa6-8525232bd48a';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure.' Silicon is a metalloid, and metalloids behave as semiconductors: their valence electrons are more tightly bound to their nuclei than those of a metallic conductor, so only a modest number are free to carry charge at room temperature. Supplying thermal energy promotes additional valence electrons into a mobile, conducting state, so a semiconductor's conductivity rises with temperature. In a true metal such as copper, conduction electrons are already delocalized, and heating mainly increases lattice vibrations that scatter them, so metallic conductivity falls with temperature, the exact opposite trend.\n(Choice A) is incorrect: it misclassifies silicon as a metal and borrows the (incorrect) idea that metallic conductivity improves on heating; a metal's delocalized electrons are already mobile, and heating lowers its conductivity through increased lattice scattering, which is why copper behaves as it does here.\n(Choice B) is incorrect: heating pure silicon over this modest range does not irreversibly break its covalent framework; the rise in conductivity comes from reversible thermal promotion of electrons across the band gap, and if bonds were permanently destroyed the effect would not reverse on cooling.\n(Choice C) is correct: it identifies silicon as a metalloid/semiconductor and gives the right mechanism: thermal energy lifts more of its tightly held valence electrons into a mobile state, increasing conductivity as temperature rises.\n(Choice D) is incorrect: the mobile charge carriers generated by heating a semiconductor are electrons (and the electron vacancies they leave behind), not free cations released by ionizing atoms; silicon atoms do not ionize into a mobile cation gas at a few hundred degrees Celsius.\nThis is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the semiconductor behavior of metalloids to explain why heating raises silicon's conductivity while lowering copper's.$px$ WHERE id = 'd90ee597-7f1a-4ae2-ae00-ea1916826867';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is B because after a carbohydrate-rich meal insulin is high, and its job is to store fuel: the liver packs glucose into glycogen and channels the surplus into fatty acids exported as VLDL, muscle pulls glucose in through GLUT4 to build glycogen and (with amino acids) protein, and adipose esterifies fatty acids into triacylglycerol. Every tissue is in net storage mode.

(Choice A) is incorrect: exporting glucose, mobilizing amino acids, and hydrolyzing triacylglycerol is the counter-regulatory (fasting) program run under low insulin and high glucagon, the opposite of the absorptive state described.

(Choice B) is correct: it correctly assigns glycogen plus lipogenesis to the liver, GLUT4 uptake for glycogen and protein synthesis to muscle, and triacylglycerol storage to adipose. That is the coordinated high-insulin response.

(Choice C) is incorrect: the well-fed liver covers its own energy needs by oxidizing surplus amino acids (sparing fat for export), not by burning fatty acids, and fed muscle synthesizes protein rather than degrading it.

(Choice D) is incorrect: under high insulin adipose stores triacylglycerol; mobilizing fatty acids to fuel the periphery is a low-insulin behavior, so this reverses the adipose response even though its liver and muscle clauses are correct.

This question tests Skill 2 (Scientific Reasoning and Problem-Solving) by making you apply insulin's integrated anabolic signal across three tissues at once rather than recall one pathway.$px$ WHERE id = 'd92bc75c-5ca7-4432-b17b-920b2d158dc0';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Biosignaling.' The answer is A because a GPCR relays its signal only after ligand binding drives a propagated conformational change that reshapes the cytoplasmic domain, and it is this reshaped surface that gains affinity for and engages the heterotrimeric G protein to switch it to the active (GTP-bound) state. (Choice A) is correct: with the ligand pocket intact but the cytoplasmic surface locked, epinephrine still binds yet the receptor cannot present the high-affinity cytoplasmic surface, so the G protein is never engaged or switched on and effector output falls to zero. (Choice B) is incorrect: it reflects the misconception that extracellular ligand binding directly activates the G protein; in fact the ligand only triggers a conformational change that must be transmitted to the cytoplasmic domain before any G protein interaction can occur. (Choice C) is incorrect: it misreads the mutation, since the stem states the extracellular pocket still binds epinephrine with normal affinity, so ligand binding is not the blocked step. (Choice D) is incorrect: it reverses the mechanism by assuming a rigid receptor stabilizes the active G protein, whereas the active state is reached by an induced conformational change, and a cytoplasmic surface that cannot be reshaped can never generate the active-state interaction at all. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the ordered causal chain of GPCR activation (ligand binding, propagated conformational change, high-affinity engagement of the G protein, and the switch to the active state) to predict the consequence of interrupting one specific step.$px$ WHERE id = 'd94015ba-5e5f-4e75-896d-950555268d4a';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Metabolism of Fatty Acids and Proteins.'

The answer is C because the enzymes of \(\beta\)-oxidation are built to act on a \(trans\)-\(\Delta 2\) enoyl-CoA, and a natural \(cis\) double bond sits at the wrong carbon and in the wrong geometry. Enoyl-CoA isomerase simply repositions it into the \(trans\)-\(\Delta 2\) form the hydratase needs. No new double bond is created. Because that double bond is already present, the round that would normally have made it via the \(\text{FAD}\)-linked dehydrogenase is bypassed, so one \(\text{FADH}_2\) is not produced and the ATP total is slightly lower.

(Choice C) is correct: the isomerase converts \(cis\) to \(trans\)-\(\Delta 2\), and skipping one \(\text{FADH}_2\)-producing step accounts for the reduced yield.
(Choice A) is incorrect: the isomerase does not cleave the chain (it only shifts a double bond), and the ATP shortfall comes from a skipped oxidation, not an extra ATP-consuming cut.
(Choice B) is incorrect: the isomerase performs no reduction and consumes no \(\text{FADH}_2\); it is a rearrangement. (The separate 2,4-dienoyl-CoA reductase used for polyunsaturates uses \(\text{NADPH}\), not \(\text{FADH}_2\).)
(Choice D) is incorrect: the double bond does not add a cycle; if anything it lets one round bypass a step, and the lost ATP is a missing \(\text{FADH}_2\), not an inefficient \(\text{NAD}^+\) step.

By reasoning from why the standard enzymes fail on a \(cis\) bond to the energetic consequence of skipping an oxidation, this item assesses Skill 2 (reasoning about scientific principles).$px$ WHERE id = 'd9a440d1-b43e-48d7-bf66-f691aa959ff6';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A protein biochemist characterizes the inter-strand hydrogen bonding in two different multi-strand \(\beta\)-sheet regions of a large protein. In region 1, the backbone hydrogen bonds are nearly linear: each carbonyl oxygen sits almost directly across from the amide hydrogen of the neighboring strand. In region 2, the corresponding hydrogen bonds are noticeably bent, so that each carbonyl oxygen is offset from the amide hydrogen it bonds to rather than aligned with it. Based on this hydrogen-bond geometry alone, which conclusion is best supported?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' In a \(\beta\)-pleated sheet, extended strands are held together by inter-strand backbone hydrogen bonds, and the geometry of those bonds depends on whether neighboring strands run in opposite directions (antiparallel) or the same direction (parallel). The source states that in the antiparallel arrangement the carbonyl O on one strand sits "exactly opposite" the amide H on the adjacent strand, giving nearly linear hydrogen bonds, whereas in a parallel sheet the hydrogen bonds are "bent significantly" because the carbonyl O is not exactly opposite the amide H; because linear hydrogen bonds are stronger, antiparallel strands are "presumably more stable." (Choice A) is correct: the nearly linear, directly-across bonds of region 1 fingerprint an antiparallel sheet (strands running in opposite directions), which is the more stable arrangement, while the bent, offset bonds of region 2 fingerprint a parallel sheet (strands running the same direction). (Choice B) is incorrect: it reverses the assignment. Linear/unbent hydrogen bonds correspond to the antiparallel (opposite-direction) sheet, not the parallel one, and the antiparallel arrangement is the more stable, so region 1 cannot be the less-stable same-direction sheet. (Choice C) is incorrect: bent inter-strand hydrogen bonds are exactly what a parallel \(\beta\)-sheet shows, so region 2 is still a sheet; the intra-strand (within a single continuous strand) hydrogen bonding that defines an \(\alpha\)-helix is a separate arrangement and is not implied by merely bent bonds between two side-by-side strands. (Choice D) is incorrect: the hydrogen-bond geometry is governed by backbone strand direction, not side-chain identity. Side chains project perpendicular to the sheet and alternate above and below the plane, so they do not set whether the carbonyl O lies directly across from the amide H. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply how backbone hydrogen-bond geometry differs between antiparallel and parallel \(\beta\)-sheets to deduce strand orientation and relative stability from experimental observations.$px$ WHERE id = 'd9ac37e5-bd0c-4972-843f-26c3c0bd06a5';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Reproductive Endocrinology (BIO)'. The answer is D because after ovulation the LH surge triggers luteinization, transforming the collapsed follicle into the corpus luteum, whose luteinized granulosa and theca cells secrete large amounts of progesterone to maintain the endometrium for a potential pregnancy. The text states that luteinization 'transforms the collapsed follicle into a new endocrine structure called the corpus luteum,' and that 'the luteinized granulosa and theca cells of the corpus luteum begin to produce large amounts of the sex steroid hormone progesterone, a hormone that is critical for the establishment and maintenance of pregnancy.' Because the patient's progesterone rises right after ovulation and supports the endometrium, the source must be the newly luteinized follicular cells, not the pituitary or a degenerating remnant. (Choice A) The anterior pituitary secretes the gonadotropins LH and FSH, not progesterone. The text notes the pituitary 'responds by secreting large amounts of LH and FSH into the bloodstream.' (Choice B) During the follicular phase the dominant follicle's granulosa and theca cells primarily produce estradiol, not progesterone; 'the release of LH also stimulates the granulosa and theca cells of the follicles to produce the sex steroid hormone estradiol.' (Choice C) The corpus albicans is the nonfunctional remnant that forms when progesterone secretion ends ('the corpus luteum will stop secreting progesterone and degrade into the corpus albicans'), so it does not sustain the endometrium. (Choice D) This correctly identifies the corpus luteum, formed by LH-driven luteinization, as the post-ovulatory source of the progesterone that maintains the endometrium. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires using the observed timing and effect of the progesterone rise to deduce which structure produces it and what endocrine event created that structure.$px$ WHERE id = 'd9bbc301-1761-4462-81ae-70bcf6c291ba';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gametogenesis & Reproductive Anatomy (BIO).' A mature sperm is regionally specialized: the head carries the haploid nucleus capped by the acrosome, the mid-piece is packed with mitochondria, and the tail contains the flagellum built around an axial filament. Each region performs one job in fertilization, so a failure in a specific fertilization step points to the region responsible for that step. Here the sperm swim and reach the oocyte normally but cannot enzymatically clear the zona pellucida, which identifies the step that is broken. (Choice A) is correct: the acrosome is the cap that 'is filled with lysosomal enzymes important for preparing sperm to participate in fertilization'; during the acrosomal reaction it 'releases its stored digestive enzymes' that 'clear a path through the zona pellucida.' Sperm that cannot release these enzymes fail exactly at the described step, so a defective acrosomal cap most directly explains the result. (Choice B) is incorrect: the compact haploid nucleus stores and delivers the paternal DNA into the oocyte after fusion; a nuclear defect would corrupt the genetic contribution, not the enzymatic penetration of the zona pellucida. (Choice C) is incorrect: the mid-piece mitochondria generate the ATP that powers the flagellum, so their failure would impair swimming and motility, but the stem specifies the sperm swim normally and reach the oocyte, ruling this out. (Choice D) is incorrect: the axial filament is the microtubule core of the flagellum built from a centriole, providing the tail's structural framework for beating; a defect there would again show up as a motility problem, not as an inability to digest the zona pellucida. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply each sperm region's specialized function to a described fertilization failure and infer which structure is responsible.$px$ WHERE id = 'd9d23647-d2b0-499e-a5fe-910870e3bd54';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$Fatty-acid oxidation strips electrons from a fatty acid, generating reduced carriers such as \(\text{NADH}\) and \(\text{FADH}_2\). Fatty-acid synthesis runs the opposite chemistry: each elongation cycle includes two reduction steps that build the fully reduced methylene carbons of the finished chain, first reducing an oxygenated (keto) carbon, and later reducing a carbon-carbon double bond. Given that synthesis must ADD electrons to build these reduced carbons, which cofactor does the pathway consume, and why?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Metabolism of Fatty Acids and Proteins.'

The answer is B because the stem says the pathway must ADD electrons to turn the intermediate carbons into fully reduced methylene carbons. Adding electrons is a reduction, and a reduction needs a reducing agent: an electron donor. The cell's dedicated reducing agent for anabolism is \(\text{NADPH}\), so fatty-acid synthesis consumes \(\text{NADPH}\), in mirror-image contrast to oxidation, which produces the reduced carriers \(\text{NADH}\) and \(\text{FADH}_2\).

(Choice B) is correct: making reduced carbons requires an electron donor, and \(\text{NADPH}\) is the reductive-biosynthesis currency that supplies those electrons.

(Choice A) is incorrect: \(\text{NAD}^+\) is an electron acceptor used to oxidize substrates; it cannot donate the electrons that a reductive synthesis step needs, and using it would run the chemistry backward.

(Choice C) is incorrect: \(\text{FADH}_2\) is a product of oxidation, not the reductant of synthesis; the synthetic reductions use \(\text{NADPH}\), and the pathway does not recycle oxidation's carriers as its electron source.

(Choice D) is incorrect: \(\text{ATP}\) contributes to committing carbon (via ACC) but cannot itself donate electrons; a reduction still requires a separate electron donor, namely \(\text{NADPH}\).

This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must reason from the redox direction of the pathway to identify the correct type of cofactor it must consume.$px$ WHERE id = 'da123749-255f-4fa1-bf1a-86b08decbeb1';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is B because oxygen crosses the respiratory membrane by simple diffusion, moving from the higher partial pressure in alveolar gas toward the lower partial pressure in capillary blood; the rate of that diffusion falls as the membrane thickens (a longer diffusion distance). In Patient 1 the alveolar PO2 is normal (105 mmHg) yet the blood leaving the alveoli is still only 70 mmHg. That wide ~35 mmHg gap means a full driving gradient was available but the blood failed to equilibrate with it. Blood that cannot pick up oxygen despite a normal alveolar partial pressure points to a barrier problem (a thickened respiratory membrane), not a gradient problem. In Patient 2 the alveolar PO2 is itself low (74 mmHg) and the blood (70 mmHg) has very nearly equilibrated with it. The membrane transferred oxygen efficiently, but there was little oxygen in the alveolus to transfer, so Patient 2's defect is a reduced alveolar-to-blood driving gradient, not a thickened membrane.
(Choice B) is correct: a persistent alveolar-to-blood PO2 difference in the face of a normal alveolar PO2 is the signature of diffusion limitation across a thickened membrane. The gradient is present, but oxygen cannot cross it fully.
(Choice A) is incorrect: the respiratory membrane does not consume oxygen; it is a passive diffusion barrier. Patient 2's low alveolar PO2 reflects too little oxygen delivered to the alveolus (poor ventilation or low inspired PO2), not metabolic consumption inside the barrier.
(Choice C) is incorrect: it reverses the physiology. A high alveolar PO2 with low blood PO2 signals that too little oxygen crossed, not too much; faster diffusion would make blood PO2 rise toward the alveolar value, not fall.
(Choice D) is incorrect: near-equilibration of blood with a low alveolar PO2 (Patient 2) indicates the membrane worked well over a normal distance; it is the reduced alveolar gradient, not a long path, that limits this patient. A long diffusion distance would leave a wide alveolar-to-blood gap, which is Patient 1's pattern.
This item tests the skill of reasoning from partial-pressure data to the underlying gas-exchange mechanism.$px$ WHERE id = 'da4adb4e-979c-4fcc-90a4-1c9e08a81b66';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).' In most prokaryotes the cell wall functions as a protective layer and is responsible for the organism's shape, and bacterial shape is not phylum-dependent: bacteria within a single phylum may be cocci, rod-shaped, or spiral. Peptidoglycan cross-linking builds the bacterial cell wall, so a compound that blocks it degrades the wall's ability to hold a non-spherical form. (Choice A) is correct: both observations point to the wall, not ancestry, as the shape-determining structure. A spherical and a spiral cell can share a phylum because shape is not phylum-dependent, and blocking peptidoglycan cross-linking removes the wall's shaping constraint so a rod collapses toward a sphere, showing an intact wall is needed to sustain an elongated shape. (Choice B) is incorrect: it treats shape as a dependable phylogenetic marker and dismisses the sequencing result, but shape is not phylum-dependent, so two differently shaped cells sharing a phylum is expected, not an error. (Choice C) is incorrect: the plasma membrane is a fluid bilayer that conforms to the cell rather than imposing a rigid form, and shape determination is assigned to the wall; the compound acts directly on wall synthesis (peptidoglycan cross-linking), not on the membrane. (Choice D) is incorrect: DNA content does not dictate external cell shape, and blocking peptidoglycan cross-linking targets wall assembly, not the chromosome, so attributing the rounding to lost genetic material misidentifies the affected structure. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principles that the cell wall determines prokaryotic shape and that shape is independent of phylogeny to interpret two experimental results rather than recall a shape term.$px$ WHERE id = 'da4d4377-d380-4ef7-8d27-5ae94643c045';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The plateau velocity approached at saturating substrate is \(V_{max}\), and the substrate concentration giving half of that plateau is \(K_m\). For any enzyme-catalyzed reaction, \(V_{max} = k_{cat}[E]_0\), so \(V_{max}\) is directly proportional to total enzyme concentration; \(K_m\) and the turnover number \(k_{cat}\) are intrinsic properties of the enzyme-substrate pair and do not depend on how much enzyme is present. (Choice A) is correct: because \(V_{max} = k_{cat}[E]_0\), doubling the total enzyme doubles the plateau velocity; \(K_m\) (units of molarity, the substrate concentration at which velocity is half of \(V_{max}\)) is a per-molecule property independent of \([E]_0\), so the half-plateau \([S]\) is essentially unchanged. (Choice B) is incorrect: it correctly scales \(V_{max}\) with enzyme amount but wrongly treats \(K_m\) as if it also scaled with enzyme concentration; \(K_m\) is set by the rate constants of the enzyme-substrate interaction, not by \([E]_0\). (Choice C) is incorrect: it holds \(V_{max}\) constant, contradicting \(V_{max} = k_{cat}[E]_0\), and invents a decrease in \(K_m\) as if more enzyme raised affinity, which it does not. (Choice D) is incorrect: it also wrongly holds \(V_{max}\) constant and, in addition, treats added enzyme as if it weakened binding (raising \(K_m\)); neither \(V_{max}\) staying fixed nor \(K_m\) changing follows from adding enzyme. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship \(V_{max} = k_{cat}[E]_0\) and the definition of \(K_m\) to predict how each parameter responds to a change in enzyme concentration.$px$ WHERE id = 'da94beb0-8f76-4672-86d1-bcfc9ebc411c';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is D because the head returns to its cocked, high-energy position only when the myosin's intrinsic ATPase hydrolyzes the bound \(\text{ATP}\) to \(\text{ADP}\) and \(\text{P}_\text{i}\), so a block confined to re-cocking must lie at that hydrolysis step. After \(\text{ATP}\) binding detaches the head from actin, "ATP is converted to ADP and Pi by the intrinsic ATPase activity of myosin," and "the energy released during ATP hydrolysis changes the angle of the myosin head into a cocked position." Because the table shows actin binding, \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) release with a normal power stroke, \(\text{ADP}\) release, and \(\text{ATP}\)-driven detachment all proceeding normally, every event upstream of re-cocking is intact; the only lesion sits at the conversion of the detached head's bound \(\text{ATP}\) into \(\text{ADP}\) and \(\text{P}_\text{i}\), which is what supplies the energy to re-cock the head for the next cycle. (Choice A) \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) release precedes and drives the power stroke ("Pi is then released, causing myosin to form a stronger attachment to the actin, after which the myosin head moves toward the M-line"), so if \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) release were blocked the power stroke would fail, yet the table shows the power stroke occurring normally. (Choice B) \(\text{ATP}\) binding is the event that detaches the head ("ATP binding causes the myosin head to detach from the actin"); detachment is recorded as normal, so this step is intact. (Choice C) \(\text{ADP}\) release happens at the end of the power stroke ("After the power stroke, ADP is released"), a step the table also marks as normal, and it occurs before \(\text{ATP}\) ever binds, so it cannot account for a failure to re-cock. (Choice D) Re-cocking is powered specifically by myosin's intrinsic ATPase hydrolyzing \(\text{ATP}\), the one step the table flags as absent, making it the precise lesion. This is a Data-based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must interpret the per-step results in the table to localize the defect to the single mechanistic step that produces the cocked position.$px$ WHERE id = 'dac926e3-1586-43d8-8d86-d19267e69cfe';
UPDATE public.questions SET options = $px$[{"text":"Hydrogen bonding between \\(\\text{CH}_3\\text{Cl}\\) molecules","label":"A"},{"text":"Dipole-dipole attraction between \\(\\text{CH}_3\\text{Cl}\\) molecules","label":"B"},{"text":"Stronger London dispersion forces in \\(\\text{CH}_3\\text{Cl}\\) due to its greater molar mass","label":"C"},{"text":"The greater strength of the polar \\(\\text{C-Cl}\\) covalent bond within \\(\\text{CH}_3\\text{Cl}\\)","label":"D"}]$px$::jsonb WHERE id = 'db10e131-c0f6-46ab-82f7-4c5f531b09bd';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' Heating disrupts the weak, noncovalent interactions (hydrogen bonds, hydrophobic contacts, van der Waals forces) that hold a protein in its native fold, producing an unfolded, inactive (denatured) state. The covalent primary structure, the peptide-bonded sequence of amino acids, is left intact. In Anfinsen's classic ribonuclease experiment, a denatured protein allowed to refold demonstrated that the primary sequence contains all the information required for the protein to achieve its native conformation. (Choice A) is correct: because the enzyme refolds and regains full activity on its own once the denaturing heat is removed, the information needed to reach the active native fold must reside in the polypeptide itself (that is, in its amino acid sequence), since nothing else was present to guide folding. (Choice B) is incorrect: molecular chaperones help prevent misfolding and aggregation in the crowded cell, but this experiment contained no chaperones or other proteins, and the enzyme still refolded, so chaperones are not required here. (Choice C) is incorrect: heat denaturation disrupts noncovalent interactions, not the covalent peptide bonds of the backbone; the primary structure is not cleaved during simple thermal denaturation, so no bonds need to "re-form" on cooling. (Choice D) is incorrect: the enzyme did lose all activity when heated, which shows the native structure was in fact disrupted (unfolded); the covalent backbone survived, but the functional folded state clearly did not. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that a protein's native fold is encoded by its primary sequence to interpret a spontaneous refolding result.$px$ WHERE id = 'db2b9f84-c963-460a-b120-64458d4b33aa';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Separations and Purifications.'

The answer is A because on an SDS-PAGE gel the logarithm of molecular weight, not molecular weight itself, is a linear function of relative migration distance. A calibration curve of \(\log(M_r)\) versus \(R_f\) is constructed from the standards, and the unknown's mass is read off (interpolated) from that line. Using the tabulated standards, the log-linear fit is approximately \(\log_{10}(M_r) = -1.64\,R_f + 5.31\). At \(R_f = 0.44\), \(\log_{10}(M_r) \approx 4.59\), so \(M_r \approx 10^{4.59} \approx 3.9 \times 10^{4}\), i.e. about 39 kDa. Equivalently, because \(R_f = 0.44\) lies between the 45.0 kDa standard (\(R_f = 0.40\)) and the 31.0 kDa standard (\(R_f = 0.50\)), interpolating on the log scale places the unknown below 45 kDa but well above 31 kDa, consistent with roughly 39 kDa.

(Choice A) is correct: it is obtained by interpolating on the logarithmic axis, which is the relationship SDS-PAGE calibration curves actually follow.

(Choice B) is incorrect: 45 kDa results from treating molecular weight itself as a linear function of \(R_f\) and fitting a straight line to the raw (untransformed) mass values, ignoring that the true relationship is linear in \(\log(M_r)\). Because the log axis compresses the larger masses, a linear-in-mass fit systematically overestimates the mass for a band in this region.

(Choice C) is incorrect: 31 kDa is the mass of the flanking standard just below the unknown (\(R_f = 0.50\)). Reporting the nearest lower band instead of interpolating rounds the estimate down and would only be correct if the unknown co-migrated with that standard, which it does not (\(R_f = 0.44 \neq 0.50\)).

(Choice D) is incorrect: about 25 kDa arises from inverting the migration measurement. For example, measuring the distance from the dye front rather than from the well effectively uses \(1 - R_f = 0.56\). Reading the curve at \(R_f = 0.56\) shifts the estimate toward the smaller, faster-migrating end and yields a mass that is too low.

To answer, you had to recognize which quantity is linearly related to \(R_f\), extract the trend from the tabulated standards, and interpolate on the correct (logarithmic) scale rather than applying a plausible-looking but incorrect linear read-off. This is reasoning from the data to the mass rather than matching a stated rule.$px$ WHERE id = 'dbef1a7f-8586-402f-acba-5439c60f1192';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is D because an enzyme lowers only the activation energy (\(E_a\)), the height of the peak between reactant and product, while leaving the reactant and product free-energy endpoints exactly where they were. Enzymes do not change the free energy of the reactants or products; they only reduce the activation energy required to reach the transition state. Because the vertical distance between the reactant and product levels is identical for the uncatalyzed and catalyzed processes, the endpoints and therefore \(\Delta G\) are unchanged, and only the activation-energy barrier shrinks. Since \(\Delta G\) is fixed by the unchanged endpoints, the enzyme changes only how FAST the reaction proceeds, not its thermodynamics. (Choice A) is incorrect: lowering the product level would change the reactant-to-product gap and therefore \(\Delta G\), but the products' free energy is not changed by the enzyme. (Choice B) is incorrect: raising the reactant level would likewise alter \(\Delta G\); the enzyme does not change the reactants' free energy. (Choice C) is incorrect: shifting both endpoints down treats the whole curve as sliding, but the endpoint-to-endpoint distance is identical in the catalyzed and uncatalyzed cases. Only the peak between them is lowered. (Choice D) is correct: only the peak (\(E_a\)) is lowered while both endpoints, and thus \(\Delta G\), stay the same. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that enzymes lower activation energy without altering reaction thermodynamics to predict how a described energy diagram changes.$px$ WHERE id = 'dc2adba0-8383-408c-a984-5260d2d37246';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Neuron & Glial Cell Structure.' Graded potentials from many synapses spread passively toward the axon hillock and initial segment, where they summate; an action potential fires only where the membrane can reach threshold and rapidly regenerate the depolarization. Summed graded potentials converge at the initial segment at the beginning of the axon to trigger the action potential, and the axon hillock is essentially this initial segment, a region carrying a high density of voltage-gated Na+ channels that drive the depolarizing phase of the action potential. (Choice A) is correct: because voltage-gated sodium channels are packed most densely here, this region has the lowest threshold, so summed inputs trigger the regenerative sodium influx of the action potential at the hillock before they can do so anywhere else. (Choice B) is incorrect: the axon hillock is not the most heavily myelinated region. It is unmyelinated, and myelin insulates the axon between nodes of Ranvier downstream; insulation would not lower threshold or explain why the impulse begins at the hillock. (Choice C) is incorrect: ligand-gated receptors are concentrated at the postsynaptic membranes of dendrites and the soma, where they produce graded potentials; they do not generate the all-or-none action potential and are not enriched at the hillock. (Choice D) is incorrect: the sodium-potassium pump restores resting gradients over time and does not open in response to depolarization, so a high pump density cannot account for rapid, threshold-triggered impulse initiation. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between voltage-gated sodium channel density and firing threshold to explain why the action potential originates at the axon hillock rather than at a more strongly stimulated dendrite.$px$ WHERE id = 'dc9aaeee-a329-4db1-93d6-8f61f958e388';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A researcher purifying an enzyme monitors purification level (fold-increase in specific activity relative to the crude lysate) after each step. Cation-exchange chromatography is run first and improves the purification level from 1 to 12. The researcher then runs the pooled active fractions over a second, identical cation-exchange column under the same buffer and gradient conditions, and observes the following:

  Step 1 (crude lysate): purification level = 1
  Step 2 (cation exchange #1): purification level = 12
  Step 3 (cation exchange #2, identical column): purification level = 13

A colleague suggests replacing the second cation-exchange step with size-exclusion chromatography, after which the purification level rises to 47. Which statement best accounts for the negligible improvement contributed by the second cation-exchange column compared with the size-exclusion column?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Separations and Purifications.'

The answer is A because each chromatographic method resolves proteins on one specific physical property, and any contaminant that behaves like the target on that property will co-elute with it. The first cation-exchange step already removed proteins whose net charge differed from the enzyme's; the contaminants that remain are precisely those that share the enzyme's charge behavior. Running a second, identical charge-based column separates on that same property again, so those co-charged contaminants elute together with the target once more and the purification level barely moves (12 to 13). Size-exclusion, by contrast, sorts on size/shape, a property independent of charge. Contaminants that are indistinguishable from the enzyme by charge are generally distinguishable by size, so the orthogonal step removes them and the purification level jumps to 47. This is the core rationale for combining orthogonal methods: each new step attacks a property the previous steps could not.

(Choice A) is correct: it identifies that the residual contaminants co-elute because they share the target's charge, and that an independent (orthogonal) property is needed to resolve them. That is exactly why the size-exclusion step succeeds where the repeated charge step fails.

(Choice B) is incorrect: while denaturation can lower specific activity, the data show the purification level rose slightly (12 to 13) rather than falling, and the subsequent size-exclusion step reached 47 using the same pooled material, so the enzyme clearly remained active. Denaturation does not explain why a repeated charge separation is intrinsically ineffective.

(Choice C) is incorrect: binding capacity governs how much protein a column can hold, not the purity achieved. Even with unlimited capacity, a second charge-based column would still fail to separate contaminants that share the target's charge, so capacity is not the limiting factor here.

(Choice D) is incorrect: size-exclusion separates by size/shape (molecular weight), not by charge, and it does not repeat the ion-exchange separation. This choice reverses the actual basis of separation and would predict no improvement, contradicting the jump to 47.

This is a Skill 1 (Knowledge of Scientific Concepts) question: it asks you to recognize the foundational principle that orthogonal purification steps separate on independent properties, and to apply that principle to interpret why a repeated method stalls while a complementary method advances purity.$px$ WHERE id = 'dcaec475-5020-41b0-a32a-0b8c945dce28';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Metabolism of Fatty Acids and Proteins.'

The answer is B because the shape of each dose-response curve identifies which step controls the rate. Palmitate oxidation is nearly zero without carnitine (6), climbs steeply as carnitine is added (40, 78), and then levels off near 97 once carnitine is no longer scarce, a classic rise-then-plateau shape. That pattern means carnitine supply sets the palmitate rate over the rising portion (it is titratable/rate-limiting), and the plateau simply marks where the carnitine-dependent transport step has saturated and some other, carnitine-independent step now caps the flux. Octanoate, by contrast, is already at ~96 with zero carnitine and does not change as carnitine is raised, so its oxidation never depends on carnitine, consistent with a medium-chain fatty acid that enters the matrix without the shuttle.

(Choice B) is correct: the rising-then-plateauing palmitate curve shows carnitine is rate-limiting up to saturation, and the flat octanoate curve shows octanoate is carnitine-independent.
(Choice A) is incorrect: it reverses the two curves. Octanoate is the flat one (already maximal at 0 carnitine), so carnitine cannot be limiting for it; palmitate is the one whose rate tracks carnitine.
(Choice C) is incorrect: a plateau is saturation, not inhibition. The palmitate rate stays high (~97) at high carnitine rather than falling, so nothing here shows carnitine inhibits oxidation once transport is saturated.
(Choice D) is incorrect: both curves plateau at essentially the same maximal rate (~97 vs ~96), so palmitate does not have a higher maximum; and octanoate reaches its maximum at zero carnitine, which directly contradicts the claim that it requires carnitine.

This item rewards Skill 4 reasoning: you must read the shape of each dose-response curve (where it starts, where it rises, and where it plateaus) to infer that carnitine is a titratable rate-limiting factor for long-chain oxidation but irrelevant to medium-chain oxidation, rather than reading any single baseline value.$px$ WHERE id = 'dcd01898-2319-490d-92ab-154e72a4fb1b';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Fetal Circulation & Birth.' Labor (parturition) is driven by a positive feedback loop: cervical stretching triggers the posterior pituitary to release oxytocin, oxytocin binds receptors on uterine smooth muscle to cause contraction, and the resulting contraction stretches the cervix further, driving still more oxytocin release. Unlike negative feedback, this self-amplifying cycle escalates until the stimulus (the fetus) is expelled. Because oxytocin acting at its uterine receptors is a required link in this cycle, blocking those receptors interrupts the loop. (Choice A) is correct: with the receptors occupied, oxytocin can no longer drive contraction, so the amplifying cycle loses its output step and the escalation stalls. This is why oxytocin-receptor blockade is used to slow labor. (Choice B) is incorrect: although the placenta does release prostaglandins that augment contractions, they act to increase contractions alongside oxytocin rather than independently powering full labor, so removing oxytocin's action still weakens the process. (Choice C) is incorrect: it treats oxytocin as an inhibitory signal, but oxytocin stimulates contraction, so blocking its receptor cannot intensify labor. This reverses the hormone's true role. (Choice D) is incorrect: it describes a negative-feedback set point returning the system to baseline, but parturition is governed by positive feedback with no such stabilizing set point. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the logic of a positive feedback loop (identifying oxytocin's uterine action as the amplifying step) to predict how a receptor blocker interrupts labor.$px$ WHERE id = 'dd1fcc4c-a667-4156-a861-a5bd6de4795b';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is B because at saturating substrate the velocity reaches its maximum, so that \(v = V_{max} = k_{cat}[E]_0\), where the catalytic rate constant \(k_{cat}\) is the turnover number, the number of substrate molecules converted to product per enzyme per second. Rearranging \(V_{max} = k_{cat}[E]_0\) gives \(k_{cat} = V_{max}/[E]_0 = (8 \times 10^{-4}\ \text{M·s}^{-1}) / (2 \times 10^{-9}\ \text{M}) = 4 \times 10^{5}\ \text{s}^{-1}\), and the \(K_m\) value is irrelevant to this calculation. (Choice A) is wrong because \(200\ \text{s}^{-1}\) comes from dividing \(V_{max}\) by \(K_m\) (\(8 \times 10^{-4} / 4 \times 10^{-6}\)); \(k_{cat}\) is normalized by enzyme concentration, not by \(K_m\), and the \(K_m\) combination instead applies to the low-substrate regime, where \(S \ll K_M\) and \(v = V_{max}S/K_M = k_{cat}[E]_0 S/K_M\). (Choice B) is correct: \(V_{max} = k_{cat}[E]_0\), so \(k_{cat} = V_{max}/[E]_0 = 4 \times 10^{5}\ \text{s}^{-1}\), consistent with \(v = V_{max} = k_{cat}[E]_0\) at saturation. (Choice C) is a partial-truth trap that reports the numeric value of \(V_{max}\) itself as the turnover number; \(V_{max}\) is a velocity that depends on enzyme concentration, whereas the turnover number is a per-enzyme first-order rate constant with units of \(\text{s}^{-1}\) obtained only after dividing out \([E]_0\), so the maximal velocity and \(k_{cat}\) are not the same quantity. (Choice D) is wrong because \(1.6 \times 10^{-12}\) comes from multiplying \([E]_0\) by \(V_{max}\) instead of dividing, which reverses the relationship \(V_{max} = k_{cat}[E]_0\) and yields a meaningless value. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the saturation relationship \(V_{max} = k_{cat}[E]_0\), rearrange it, carry out a unit-aware division, and reject the partial truth that \(V_{max}\) is itself the turnover number to arrive at \(k_{cat}\).$px$ WHERE id = 'dd273d94-e462-4bf0-bc70-7ac1e869c515';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is D because only a few hours into a fast the fastest way to defend blood glucose is to release glucose that is already stored: glucagon activates glycogen phosphorylase, so hepatic glycogen is broken down almost immediately, whereas building new glucose by gluconeogenesis ramps up more slowly.

(Choice A) is incorrect: gluconeogenesis is stimulated in early fasting but responds comparatively slowly, so it cannot yet be the dominant source. This choice reverses the true speed ranking.

(Choice B) is incorrect: skeletal muscle lacks glucose-6-phosphatase, so muscle glycogen serves the muscle itself and cannot release free glucose into the blood.

(Choice C) is incorrect: fatty acids are not converted to glucose in humans, and ketone production is minimal this early; lipolysis at this stage mainly supplies fuel and gluconeogenic glycerol, not blood glucose directly.

(Choice D) is correct: mobilizing existing hepatic glycogen requires only enzyme activation, making it the first and fastest defense of blood glucose in the hours after a meal.

This item rewards Skill 2 (Scientific Reasoning and Problem-Solving): you must reason about the relative speed of two glucose sources within a defined metabolic window rather than restate which pathway exists.$px$ WHERE id = 'dd49a9b9-24aa-4943-9e51-0f6fbecd070b';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gastrulation & Germ Layers.' During organogenesis, the mesoderm-derived notochord lies beneath the dorsal ectoderm and provides the inductive signal that causes the overlying ectoderm to form the neural plate and then roll up into the neural tube; in vertebrates the notochord is present only transiently during embryonic development, and while it is present it induces formation of the neural tube. Removing the inducer before neurulation therefore removes the signal that the ectoderm depends on. (Choice A) is correct: without the notochord's inductive signal, the overlying ectoderm has nothing to trigger neural-plate formation and folding, so the neural tube (and the brain and spinal cord it would have produced) fails to form at that site. (Choice B) is incorrect: neurulation is not autonomous; signaling determines which ectoderm becomes neural tissue, and the notochord is the source of that induction, so folding does not proceed on its own once the inducer is gone. (Choice C) is incorrect: the notochord does help pattern the paraxial mesoderm, but its role is not limited to that. It is precisely the neural tube that depends on notochord induction, so predicting a normal tube reverses the actual dependency. (Choice D) is incorrect: the gut is the endoderm-derived digestive tube, and its closure does not depend on a dorsal notochord signal; the notochord's inductive target is the ectoderm above it, not the endoderm below. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the notochord's role as the inducer of the neural tube to predict the outcome of removing it before neurulation.$px$ WHERE id = 'dd63b20b-e4b7-4dbb-87d6-c0ab3a123274';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO).' At anaphase, sister chromatids are separated and each is pulled toward the pole whose spindle microtubules are attached to its kinetochore; the metaphase checkpoint normally verifies that a chromosome's two kinetochores are bound to opposite poles before separation is permitted (lines 5481-5484, 5527-5529). When both kinetochores of one chromosome are captured by the same pole and the checkpoint is silenced, the shortening microtubules drag both chromatids in the same direction. (Choice A) is correct: because both kinetochores point to one pole, anaphase pulls both sister chromatids toward that single pole, so one daughter cell inherits two copies of the chromosome and the other inherits zero. That is a nondisjunction-type mis-segregation that the checkpoint exists to prevent. (Choice B) is incorrect: normal one-copy-per-daughter segregation requires the two kinetochores to face opposite poles, which is exactly the condition that fails here, so proceeding into anaphase cannot yield an even split for this chromosome. (Choice C) is incorrect: anaphase separation reflects loss of centromeric cohesion, not the direction of attachment, so the chromatids do separate at the centromere; misattachment changes where they go, not whether they split. (Choice D) is incorrect: the condensation state of chromatin is governed by phase progression (chromosomes decondense in telophase, lines 5487-5489) and is independent of which pole a chromatid is pulled toward, so faulty pole attachment would not leave delivered chromosomes permanently condensed. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the kinetochore-to-pole attachment mechanism to a checkpoint-failure scenario and predict how mis-oriented attachment redistributes chromosomes to the daughter cells.$px$ WHERE id = 'de02cc30-990c-4a7f-bab0-2ec89a179762';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions.' Formal charge is a bookkeeping tool calculated as the number of valence electrons on the neutral atom minus the nonbonding electrons minus the number of bonds to that atom: \(\text{FC} = V - N - B\). For the terminal oxygen in this cyanate structure, neutral oxygen has \(6\) valence electrons, the three lone pairs contribute \(6\) nonbonding electrons, and there is \(1\) single bond to carbon. (Choice B) is correct: applying the formula gives \(\text{FC} = 6 - 6 - 1 = -1\), which also matches the requirement that the formal charges over the whole ion must sum to the \(-1\) ionic charge (here N and C each carry \(0\)). (Choice A) is incorrect: a formal charge of \(0\) is what oxygen carries only when it has two lone pairs and two bonds (as in a neutral C=O), but here the single-bonded oxygen with three lone pairs has one extra nonbonding pair, making it electron-rich relative to neutral oxygen. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(+1\) reverses the sign of the bookkeeping. It would arise from subtracting valence electrons from the assigned electrons rather than the other way around, or from giving oxygen too few nonbonding electrons; oxygen here has more electrons than a neutral atom, so it must be negative, not positive. (Choice D) is incorrect: \(-2\) double-counts by treating the shared bonding pair as if both electrons belonged solely to oxygen (\(6 - 8 = -2\)); in formal-charge bookkeeping each atom is assigned only one electron from each bond, so the bond counts as \(1\), not \(2\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the formal-charge formula to a specified Lewis structure rather than recall a memorized value.$px$ WHERE id = 'de376b50-fa62-4c15-9798-bfd3ee16e7c0';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gastrulation & Germ Layers.' During organogenesis, the ectoderm gives rise to two things: the epidermis and the nervous system. Growth factors signal the cells at the edge of the ectoderm to become epidermis, while the remaining cells in the center form the neural plate, which rolls into the neural tube and becomes the brain and spinal cord; critically, if this growth-factor signaling is disrupted, the entire ectoderm differentiates into neural tissue. (Choice A) is correct: because the epidermis is the induced fate that requires the margin growth-factor signal, removing that signal leaves neural tissue as the default fate, so nearly the whole ectoderm differentiates into neural tissue with little or no epidermis. (Choice B) is incorrect: this reverses the induction logic. Epidermis is the fate that depends on the growth factor, so losing the signal reduces epidermis rather than expanding it, and neural tissue is the pathway that proceeds without it. (Choice C) is incorrect: somites, vertebrae, ribs, and skeletal muscle are mesodermal derivatives, not ectodermal, so no ectodermal manipulation redirects this layer into somites. (Choice D) is incorrect: the columnar epithelium lining the digestive tract and internal organs derives from endoderm, a different germ layer that the ectoderm does not switch into. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the rule that epidermis is the growth-factor-induced fate and neural tissue is the default fate to predict the outcome of removing the inducing signal.$px$ WHERE id = 'de4796ef-9376-4a23-aa90-6228591e6106';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Titration (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because at the equivalence point of a monoprotic strong acid-strong base titration the moles of added \(\text{OH}^-\) exactly equal the moles of \(\text{H}^+\) initially present, so \(M_\text{acid}V_\text{acid} = M_\text{base}V_\text{base}\). Compute the moles of acid: \((0.100\ \text{M})(40.0\ \text{mL}) = 4.00\ \text{mmol}\ \text{HCl}\). At equivalence this requires \(4.00\ \text{mmol}\ \text{NaOH}\). Solving for the base volume, \(V_\text{base} = \dfrac{M_\text{acid}V_\text{acid}}{M_\text{base}} = \dfrac{(0.100)(40.0)}{0.200} = 20.0\ \text{mL}\). Because the base is twice as concentrated as the acid, it takes half the acid's volume to neutralize it. (Choice A) 10.0 mL comes from dividing the 20.0 mL result by an extra factor of two, as if the base were four times as concentrated as the acid; only one factor of \(\tfrac{0.200}{0.100} = 2\) applies, giving 20.0 mL, not 10.0 mL. (Choice C) 40.0 mL assumes the equivalence point occurs when equal volumes of acid and base are mixed, which is true only for equal concentrations; here \(M_\text{base} = 0.200\ \text{M} \neq M_\text{acid} = 0.100\ \text{M}\), so equal volumes would deliver twice the needed \(\text{OH}^-\). (Choice D) 80.0 mL comes from inverting the formula and computing \(\dfrac{M_\text{base}V_\text{acid}}{M_\text{acid}} = \dfrac{(0.200)(40.0)}{0.100}\); this misplaces the base molarity in the numerator, whereas the more concentrated base must require a smaller, not larger, volume. Skill 2$px$ WHERE id = 'deca58de-5745-4470-b4cc-e3e87dd7edc6';
UPDATE public.questions SET options = $px$[{"label":"A","text":"His seminiferous tubules and ductus deferens fail to form, because testosterone is required to build this duct anatomy at puberty."},{"label":"B","text":"FSH secretion from his anterior pituitary collapses, removing the pituitary signal that produces the male secondary sex characteristics."},{"label":"C","text":"His voice deepens and facial hair appears on schedule, but his libido and sperm production are the only functions that fail to develop."},{"label":"D","text":"The secondary sex characteristics testosterone normally drives (voice deepening, facial and body hair, and muscle and skeletal growth) fail to develop."}]$px$::jsonb WHERE id = 'defe901d-26f1-4af2-954b-d817847d1ba5';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Oxidative Phosphorylation.'

The answer is D because a permanently open proton channel that bypasses the catalytic head uncouples electron transport from ATP synthesis. Protons that the electron transport chain pumps into the intermembrane space now leak back through the defective channel without turning the catalytic machinery, so the proton-motive force is dissipated as heat rather than captured as \(\text{ATP}\). Because the gradient never builds up to oppose further pumping, respiratory control is lost and the chain speeds up: it consumes more \(\text{O}_2\) (the terminal electron acceptor) in an effort to rebuild the gradient. The result is high \(\text{O}_2\) consumption, low \(\text{ATP}\) output, and heat. That is the classic uncoupled state.

(Choice D) is correct: dissipating the gradient without doing phosphorylation work makes the electron transport chain run faster (more \(\text{O}_2\) used) while \(\text{ATP}\) synthesis drops and the energy exits as heat.
(Choice A) is incorrect: it wrongly assumes \(\text{O}_2\) use and \(\text{ATP}\) output stay coupled; the whole point of the leaky channel is that protons return without driving the catalytic head, so \(\text{ATP}\) synthesis falls even as respiration rises.
(Choice B) is incorrect: it reverses the effect on the chain. Collapsing the gradient removes the back-pressure that slows pumping, so the chain accelerates rather than halting; a chain halt would instead follow blocking a complex or removing \(\text{O}_2\).
(Choice C) is incorrect: even though the catalytic head is structurally intact, ATP synthesis requires proton flow to be routed through that head to drive its conformational cycle; when protons short-circuit past it, the head has no energy source and cannot phosphorylate \(\text{ADP}\) at the normal rate.

This item rewards Skill 2 reasoning: rather than recalling which fragment is the channel, you trace how a specific defect in proton coupling propagates through the chemiosmotic mechanism to predict the effects on respiration, ATP yield, and heat.$px$ WHERE id = 'df108a7c-6b9f-486d-8226-ca54081ca640';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$An investigator records plasma insulin and glucagon in one subject at two moments:

| Time point | Insulin | Glucagon | Insulin-to-glucagon ratio |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| I (after a meal) | 60 µU/mL | 40 pg/mL | high |
| II (18-hour fast) | 5 µU/mL | 120 pg/mL | low |

Hepatic glycogen synthase and glycogen phosphorylase are under reciprocal covalent control, with phosphorylation activating phosphorylase and inactivating synthase. Using the data at time point II, which prediction of the two enzymes' activities and the net direction of glycogen metabolism is correct?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is C because at time point II the insulin-to-glucagon ratio is low, meaning glucagon dominates the liver. Glucagon acts on the hepatocyte through cAMP to drive phosphorylation of the glycogen-handling enzymes, and the stem states that phosphorylation activates glycogen phosphorylase and inactivates glycogen synthase. So at time point II phosphorylase is active and synthase is inactive, and the liver is net breaking glycogen down (glycogenolysis). That is the expected response to an 18-hour fast.

(Choice A) is incorrect: a low insulin-to-glucagon ratio means glucagon, not insulin, dominates (insulin is the numerator, so a low ratio reflects low insulin relative to glucagon). The active-synthase, inactive-phosphorylase storage pattern belongs to the high-ratio fed state at time point I, not to the fasting time point II.

(Choice C) is correct: the low ratio makes glucagon dominant, phosphorylation activates phosphorylase and inactivates synthase, and the net direction is glycogen breakdown.

(Choice B) is incorrect: it correctly has glucagon phosphorylating the enzymes but then flips the effect the stem specifies. Phosphorylation activates phosphorylase and inactivates synthase, so the result is breakdown, not storage.

(Choice D) is incorrect: reciprocal covalent control holds the two enzymes in opposite activity states, so they are not both active. Simultaneous full synthesis and breakdown would be a futile cycle, which the covalent switch is designed to avoid.

This question rewards Skill 4 (data-based reasoning): you had to read the insulin-to-glucagon ratio from the table, decide which hormone dominates, and apply the stated phosphorylation rule to predict each enzyme's activity and the net direction of glycogen metabolism.$px$ WHERE id = 'df7da62d-1ab6-4931-9210-0af1efbe045b';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC).' A reaction is classified as oxidation-reduction (redox) when one or more elements change oxidation number, and as single-displacement when one element in solution is replaced by a free element that is itself oxidized. Assigning oxidation numbers here: zinc goes from \(0\) in \(\text{Zn}(s)\) to \(+2\) in \(\text{ZnSO}_4\); copper goes from \(+2\) in \(\text{CuSO}_4\) to \(0\) in \(\text{Cu}(s)\); sulfur stays \(+6\) and oxygen stays \(-2\) inside the unchanged sulfate ion. (Choice A) is correct: free zinc replaces copper in solution (single displacement), and because zinc's oxidation number rises \(0 \rightarrow +2\) (oxidized) while copper's falls \(+2 \rightarrow 0\) (reduced), electrons are transferred, making it a redox reaction. (Choice B) is incorrect: the reaction is not double displacement, since a free element (\(\text{Zn}\)) is involved rather than two ionic compounds swapping partners, and sulfur does not change. It remains \(+6\) in the intact sulfate on both sides. (Choice C) is incorrect: although the sulfate ion truly is a spectator that stays intact, zinc and copper both change oxidation number, so electron transfer does occur and the reaction is definitely redox. (Choice D) is incorrect: combustion requires rapid reaction with an oxidant (typically \(\text{O}_2\)) producing heat and light, which is absent here, and the oxidation-number changes are reversed: copper is reduced (\(+2 \rightarrow 0\)) and zinc is oxidized (\(0 \rightarrow +2\)), not the other way around. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply oxidation-number assignments to a novel reaction to determine both the displacement pattern and whether electron transfer occurs.$px$ WHERE id = 'dfd24d6c-747e-4f64-ba75-ba55675c4ca5';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is B because monosaccharides are named by combining the type of carbonyl group with the number of carbons in the chain. D-ribose has a five-carbon backbone, and its carbonyl is a terminal \(\text{-CHO}\) group (a carbon double-bonded to oxygen and bonded to one hydrogen), which is an aldehyde. A five-carbon sugar bearing an aldehyde is therefore an aldopentose, the class to which D-ribose belongs.

(Choice B) is correct: ribose has five carbons (the "pent-" root) and a terminal aldehyde carbonyl (the "aldo-" prefix), making it an aldopentose.

(Choice A) is incorrect: "hex-" designates a six-carbon backbone, as in glucose (an aldohexose). Ribose has one fewer carbon than that, so calling it an aldohexose overcounts the skeleton by one carbon.

(Choice C) is incorrect: the "keto-" prefix requires a ketone carbonyl, in which the carbonyl carbon is bonded to two other carbons (as in fructose). Ribose's carbonyl carbon instead carries a hydrogen, marking it an aldehyde, so a ketopentose classification misreads the carbonyl even though the "pent-" count is right.

(Choice D) is incorrect: "tetr-" designates a four-carbon backbone. Ribose's chain is longer than that, so aldotetrose undercounts the skeleton, even though it correctly recognizes the carbonyl as an aldehyde.

This is a Skill 1 (Knowledge of Scientific Concepts) item: it asks you to apply the standard carbohydrate naming convention by recalling the carbon count and carbonyl type of a common monosaccharide.$px$ WHERE id = 'dff2b230-3ecd-412e-8669-6909640cf8f3';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$Ordinarily a buffer is most effective only when the surrounding pH is within about one unit of its pKa. The bicarbonate system (CO2 + H2O ⇌ H2CO3 ⇌ H+ + HCO3−) has a pKa near 6.1, yet it is the body's most important buffer at a physiological pH of about 7.4, which is more than a full unit away. Which feature of this system best explains why it buffers so effectively despite this mismatch?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is D because the bicarbonate system is an 'open' buffer: added H+ is consumed by HCO3− to form H2CO3 and then CO2, and because the lungs continuously exhale that CO2, the acid product is removed from the system instead of accumulating. In a closed buffer, accumulating conjugate acid would drive the equilibrium back and exhaust buffering capacity as pH moves away from the pKa; ventilation-adjustable CO2 removal frees the bicarbonate system from that limitation, which is why it remains effective even though its pKa (~6.1) lies more than a unit below physiological pH. (Choice D) is correct: it identifies the open, ventilation-coupled removal of CO2 as the reason the system buffers effectively despite the pKa mismatch. (Choice A) is incorrect: the equilibrium constant for CO2 hydration is actually small (the reaction favors dissolved CO2), and no chemistry lets a finite amount of buffer neutralize 'unlimited' acid without changing gas exchange. The answer even contradicts the open-system mechanism by denying any role for ventilation. (Choice B) is incorrect: dissolved CO2 does not shift the intrinsic pKa to 7.4; the pKa remains ~6.1, and the system's effectiveness comes from CO2 removal, not from the buffer sitting at its maximum-capacity point. (Choice C) is incorrect: at physiological pH bicarbonate is present in large excess over carbonic acid (roughly 20:1), not the reverse, and buffering of added acid is done by that bicarbonate reserve consuming H+, not by an excess of H2CO3 neutralizing base. This question tests the skill of reasoning about how the open, ventilation-coupled nature of the CO2/bicarbonate system extends its buffering capacity beyond the usual pKa ± 1 rule.$px$ WHERE id = 'e0530a72-b56a-4d65-b9dd-f8e325c050b9';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Fetal Circulation & Birth.' Because the fetal lungs are compressed and nonfunctional, blood follows the path of least resistance away from the high-resistance pulmonary bed, and three shunts route it accordingly: the foramen ovale moves blood between the atria, the ductus arteriosus moves blood between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta, and the ductus venosus lets placental blood bypass the liver. Identifying the correct shunt requires matching each vessel to the exact pair of structures it connects. (Choice A) is correct: the ductus arteriosus is a short, muscular vessel that connects the pulmonary trunk to the aorta, so most of the blood pumped from the right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk is diverted into the aorta. That is exactly the flow the stem describes, driven by the high resistance of the collapsed pulmonary bed. (Choice B) is incorrect: it names the correct pulmonary-trunk-to-aorta connection but assigns it to the wrong structure; the foramen ovale is an opening in the interatrial septum, not a vessel between the great arteries. (Choice C) is incorrect: this accurately describes the foramen ovale, which allows blood to flow from the right atrium to the left atrium, but that shunt bypasses the lungs at the atrial level and does not handle blood already ejected into the pulmonary trunk. (Choice D) is incorrect: the ductus venosus truly connects the umbilical vein to the inferior vena cava, letting oxygenated placental blood bypass the fetal liver, so it is unrelated to diverting pulmonary-trunk blood into the aorta. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the resistance principle and the distinct connections of each shunt to determine which one accounts for the described pulmonary-trunk-to-aorta flow, rather than recalling a single stated fact.$px$ WHERE id = 'e07f8f08-33c9-40c1-a46c-a278bca18221';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Mechanisms of Development.' The four categories of chemical signaling differ mainly in how far the signal travels and how it gets from source to target: paracrine signals diffuse locally through the extracellular matrix to nearby cells, endocrine signals are hormones carried long distances by the bloodstream, autocrine signals act back on the releasing cell, and gap-junction signals pass directly between adjacent cytoplasms without entering the extracellular space. Chemical synaptic transmission is itself an example of paracrine signaling, because the neurotransmitter is a secreted ligand that diffuses across the narrow cleft and is then quickly degraded or reabsorbed. (Choice A) is correct: the classification rests on the mechanism of transport, not on the cell type involved. Because the neurotransmitter is a secreted molecule that diffuses through the extracellular space to a neighboring cell, synaptic transmission falls under paracrine signaling, the same general category as other short-range secreted messengers, rather than a class of its own. (Choice B) is incorrect: although a neurotransmitter is a released chemical messenger, endocrine signaling specifically requires a hormone carried via the bloodstream over large distances, where it becomes diluted; the messenger here crosses only a ~30 nm gap, so distance and transport route rule endocrine out. (Choice C) is incorrect: autocrine signaling requires that the signaling cell and the target cell be the same, but the scenario states the neurotransmitter acts on a distinct downstream nerve cell across the cleft, not back on the releasing terminal. (Choice D) is incorrect: gap-junction signaling moves mediators directly between cells through channels linking their cytoplasms, whereas the neurotransmitter here is released into and diffuses across the extracellular cleft before binding surface receptors. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the criteria that distinguish the four signaling categories to a described mechanism and infer the correct classification, rather than recalling a definition that is handed to you.$px$ WHERE id = 'e0c77e2f-aa73-4846-b3f0-2f0c44216866';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$During a near-miss traffic accident, a surge of epinephrine, a catecholamine, floods the driver's bloodstream as part of the fight-or-flight response. The same stressor also raises cortisol. Which statement best predicts the tempo and tissue pattern by which epinephrine mobilizes fuel?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is A because epinephrine is a water-soluble catecholamine that binds cell-surface (beta-adrenergic) receptors and raises cAMP. A second-messenger cascade needs no new gene transcription, so it acts within seconds (the tempo demanded by fight-or-flight), and it simultaneously activates glycogen phosphorylase in the liver and hormone-sensitive lipase in adipose, releasing glucose and free fatty acids at the same time. That fast, cAMP-driven, multi-tissue catabolic profile is the reasoning the scenario asks you to predict.

(Choice A) is correct: a cell-surface receptor plus cAMP means no transcription is required, so fuel is mobilized in seconds, and the catecholamine drives hepatic glycogenolysis and adipose lipolysis together, exactly the pattern that fits an acute stressor.
(Choice B) is incorrect: an hours-long, transcription-dependent tempo describes a slow steroid such as cortisol, which the same stressor also releases; a water-soluble amine cannot act through gene induction and would be far too slow to power fight-or-flight.
(Choice C) is incorrect: the fast cAMP mechanism is right, but restricting fuel release to the hepatocyte describes glucagon; a catecholamine also mobilizes adipose fat (and skeletal-muscle glycogen), so leaving adipose untouched contradicts its role.
(Choice D) is incorrect: the tempo is right, but the direction is reversed. Epinephrine mobilizes (releases) fuel into the blood, whereas driving glucose and fatty acids into storage is the anabolic, insulin-directed fed-state response.

This question tests Skill 2 (Scientific Reasoning and Problem-Solving) by asking you to reason from a hormone's chemical class to the tempo and tissue pattern of its metabolic action, and to contrast it with the slower steroid response, rather than recalling where it is made.$px$ WHERE id = 'e14f89fc-d952-464c-a93b-cd8567caba4b';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)'. The answer is A because synapsis (the tight, full-length pairing of homologous chromosomes mediated by the synaptonemal complex) occurs in prophase I but never in mitotic prophase. The text states that early in prophase I "The synaptonemal complex, a lattice of proteins between the homologous chromosomes, first forms at specific locations and then spreads outward to cover the entire length of the chromosomes. The tight pairing of the homologous chromosomes is called synapsis." Crucially, the same passage notes, "Recall that in mitosis, homologous chromosomes do not pair together," so synapsis is the defining feature that separates the two cells. The other listed events either also occur in mitosis or are not the homolog-pairing event being asked about. (Choice A) is correct: homolog-to-homolog synapsis along the full length is unique to prophase I and is absent from mitosis, where homologs stay independent. (Choice B) is wrong because cohesin holding sister chromatids together at the centromere is real, but sister-chromatid cohesion also occurs in mitosis, so it cannot be the distinguishing event. (Choice C) is wrong because tip attachment to the nuclear envelope, while a genuine prophase-I detail, is not the homolog-pairing event the question targets. (Choice D) is wrong because spindle attachment to kinetochores is the key event of prometaphase, not the synapsis event of prophase I. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires comparing two cell types and reasoning out which single chromosomal event is uniquely diagnostic of meiotic prophase I rather than simply recalling a definition.$px$ WHERE id = 'e1a9d35f-a058-409c-844e-a04eb67e625f';

COMMIT;
